\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Mizoguchi/Too Much-2: After Dlr Fall To Y103.20 \b0\par\par\uc2 161 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 02:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the international bureau at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Thursday the recent sharp rise in the yen has been "too much" and that Japan will take appropriate measures to counter the move. \par \par \uc2  "I think the recent yen strengthening has been too sudden," Mizoguchi told reporters upon his arrival at the ministry, adding that Japanese authorities are watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closely. \par \par \uc2  When asked whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, Mizoguchi said, "We will act appropriately in line with market moves." \par \par \uc2  He also noted that the U.S. policy supporting a strong dollar hasn't changed. \par \par \uc2  At 2352 GMT (7:52 p.m. EDT Wednesday), the dollar was trading around Y104.90, after dropping as low as Y103.20 in overseas markets Wednesday, its weakest level against the yen since January 1996. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0005GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9g06s7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
