\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo traders unimpressed with rare BOJ-MOF meet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 11:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar took a renewed dive against the yen late on Thursday with traders seeing little of substance from a rare session between Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami on the yen's recent sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  Prior to the meeting the dollar rose to as high as 105.60 yen, but fell sharply following the meeting between Miyazawa and Hayami and fell as low as 103.57 yen as of 0837 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers who had bought the dollar on speculation that there may be some type of announcement on unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidated their long dollar positions after Miyazawa said no he did not discuss leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilised. \par \par \uc2  Such a move would effectively inject more cash into the banking system and represent a new way to ease monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa and Hayami met for about an hour in a Tokyo hotel from 4.15 p.m. (0715 GMT) to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other issues. \par \par \uc2  Following the meeting, Miyazawa told reporters he expressed his concern to Hayami about the effects of the yen's rise on the economy while Hayami said he agreed with Miyazawa on the need to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "We exchanged views, that was the purpose of our meeting... We also discussed recent market movements and their effect on Japan's economy," Miyazawa said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the dollar fell as the market was disappointed by Miyazawa and Hayami's insistence that they held no specific discussions about further steps the BOJ could take to ease monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "There is very little that is new in their comments," said Hank Note, assistant general manager at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar might have been bought if Hayami had made any comments which left open the possibility of a further monetary easing, but his insistence that the zero interest rate policy was sufficient was negative for the dollar, Note said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether he and Miyazawa discussed steps to further ease monetary policy or to allow unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hayami said: "We did not discuss such specific issues concerning monetary policy." The BOJ's present policy of guiding short-term rates to virtually zero is more than sufficient to provide ample funds for the money market, Hayami added. \par \par \uc2  Yasuji Yamanaka, chief manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Nikko Trust and Banking Corp, said Miyazawa's comments on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were too vague to curb the market's appetite for yen. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said it was best that he not comment on the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said he could not say whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be one of the main topics at the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting to be held around September 25. \par \par \uc2  Nikko Trust's Yamanaka said the dollar would probably have to fall a bit more for the United States to agree to joint dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Most of the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  done by the United States in the past were conducted at dollar rates below 100 yen. So the dollar would have to fall a bit more before the risk of a joint U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases," Yamanaka said. \par \par \uc2  "Unless the yen's strength against the dollar results in a heavy repatriation of Japanese money from the United States and a drop in U.S. asset prices, the United States will probably remain reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yamanaka added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that speculation of further credit easing measures by the Bank of Japan could linger ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next policy board meeting on September 21, supporting the dollar and Japanese government bonds. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ could be cornered into taking some monetary action next week ahead of the G7 meeting" if the yen were to rise further ahead of the policy board meeting, said Masaru Hamasaki, manager of treasury division at Zenshinren Bank. \par \par \uc2  Economists, however, scoffed at the idea that a further credit easing would have a tangible impact on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Calls to prop up the dollar via unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or increases in the BOJ's outright bond purchases are "sheer nonsense," said Yasushi Okada, chief economist for Credit Suisse First Boston Securities (Japan) Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Japanese money could continue to stay home or even repatriate more from the United States as long as worries over the explosive U.S. balance of payment deficit linger, even if such policies were to lead to a wider interest rate differential between Japan and the United States, Okada said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9g00liy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
