\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seesaws in U.S., market eyes yen moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar seesawed against the yen as U.S. trading got underway on Thursday, pushed around by speculation Japan may soon ask its international partners for help in taming the yen's newfound strength. \par \par \uc2  Traders remained on red alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and predicted wide price swings as the market attempts to consolidate some its gains. The yen had jumped over seven percent since September 9, when Japan released fresh strong growth data. \par \par \uc2  The dollar started at 104.14/24 yen, below Wednesday's 104.66/76 yen closing level, but quickly bounced above 105 yen as a large U.S. financial institution was said to be buying yen. \par \par \uc2  "Right now people are scratching their heads, realising the market is ripe for a turnaround. People are trying to be flat and we could see broad swings which may not mean anything," Citibank analyst T.J. Marta explained. \par \par \uc2  An overnight meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami plus news that Miyazawa's top deputy Haruhiko Kuroda will travel to the United States sparked the yen's sharp moves. \par \par \uc2  While the Japanese government says a rise in the yen is generally a sign of a welcome "buy Japan" mentality, it also fears the yen's recent sharp surge may smother the nascent recovery that has sparked the buying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar quickly jumped above 105 yen overnight when traders hoped the meeting would produce fresh tactics to tame yen strength. But when officials stopped short of suggesting a shift in policy, the yen regained the upper hand. \par \par \uc2  "What is yet to be seen is whether the U.S. Treasury or the G7 more broadly will support joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," analysts at BankBoston said. \par \par \uc2  As long as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to sterilise its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by effectively withdrawing the money it has pumped into the system through the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers predicted Washington's policy will remain hands off. \par \par \uc2  Trading may also become more volatile due to local weather conditions, dealers said, noting several markets have been advised to close in early afternoon as New York faces Hurricane Floyd. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the brokers go home, the machines are still on," creating another variable for an already hypersensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, ING Barings senior vice president John McCarthy said. \par \par \uc2  With the storm hurtling up the East Coast there is some "squaring up which in this context means selling yen and buying euros," Mellon Bank chief strategist Marc Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  The euro gained modestly against the dollar, rising to $1.0409/14 from its starting level of $1.0379/86. On Wednesday, the euro closed at $1.0405/10. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also slipped to 1.5375/85 Swiss francs from its starting level around 1.5432/42 after closing at 1.5408/18 on Wednesday. The British pound continues to advance, rising to $1.6245/55 from $1.6163/73 at the start which was up from Wednesday's $1.6140/50 close. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar slipped to $0.6466/76 at the start of trading from $0.6491/96 on Wednesday and the dollar rose against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4773/83 from C$1.4752/62. \par \par \uc2  ((North American Treasury Desk, (212) 859-1639)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9g00l92\par }\page {\par\fs20{\