\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr gets brief lift vs yen on news of BOJ-MOF meet. \b0\par\par\uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar staged a short-lived bounce against the yen in late Tokyo on Thursday as news of a meeting between the heads of Japan's Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised speculation of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) said Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami would meet at 4:15 p.m. (0715 GMT) to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other issues. \par \par \uc2  Neither the ministry nor the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they planned to brief reporters on the meeting. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to around 105.60 yen in an initial reaction to the news, but by 0635 GMT it had fallen back to 104.85/90 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had stood at 104.66/76 yen in late New York on Wednesday. Tokyo markets were closed on Wednesday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0453/58 at 0649 GMT, compared with $1.0405/10 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The news of the meeting at a Tokyo hotel between the BOJ and MOF heads fuelled speculation the BOJ may be persuaded to take more credit-easing steps to help dampen the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A city bank dealer said: "It now seems there is more chance of a BOJ easing at the next board meeting on September 21. If that is the case, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen may follow to back up the policy change." \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar tumbled as far as around 103.20 yen in London, its lowest level since December 1995 and down about 17 percent from this year's high of 124.75 yen in May. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar regained ground later in New York trade, partly due to a report in a Japanese newspaper saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities have started discussing joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their U.S. and European counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The market became increasing wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Miyazawa said on Thursday morning that his deputy, Haruhiko Kuroda, was in Washington analysing recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions with officials from other nations. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said there was a need to exchange views on the situation with other countries and that Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, had gone from New York to Washington to discuss the situation with officials there. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market remained sceptical over whether the United States would agree to coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the yen's rise has not caused instability in U.S. financial markets. \par \par \uc2  But they said the BOJ could still buy dollars for yen via other central banks in overseas markets as it did in the last series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  which ended on July 21. \par \par \uc2  In its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ sold yen for dollars last Friday and this Tuesday, but its efforts were overwhelmed by strong yen buying led by overseas fund operators. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's rise to 105.60 yen (after the news of a meeting between Miyazawa and Hayami) was capped by heavy selling by U.S. operators," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, traders cited a sharp drop in Tokyo stocks as one factor supporting the dollar and protecting its floor of 104.40 yen during the Tokyo session. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei's slide fuelled the view that foreign investors may sell yen after collecting profits from stocks, while potential dollar sellers such as Japanese investors and exporters were widely seen sidelined on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average of 225 leading shares ended Thursday down 485.63 points or 2.73 percent at 17,291.59, after losing more than four percent at one point in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which tumbled to a fresh lifetime low of 106.90 yen overnight, stood at 109.81/95 yen at 0750 GMT, compared with 108.98 yen in late New York on Wednesday. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.news room@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9g00k5h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
