\par\par \b \uc2  As Yen Rally Gathers Momentum, New Threats To Dlr Emerge \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Joncus \par \uc2 1,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 15:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar and the euro's slide against the yen gathered momentum Wednesday morning in Europe, as both fell by between two and three yen to hit fresh multi-year lows. \par \par \uc2  That amounted to cumulative losses of between five and six yen just half-way through the week. If extended through to the end of the week, the trend would put the dollar under Y98. \par \par \uc2  Market participants say that is unlikely, because the dynamic of the yen's latest rally is not one of fundamental flows into Japanese markets, as it was earlier. More than anything else, the yen has been driven upward this week by its own previous gains. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think anyone wants to buy yen below Y105; you could say that people are being forced to buy yen," said Gerry Celaya, a senior analyst at American Express Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  But although the yen rally may have gotten ahead of fundamentals for now, over the medium term analysts see accumulating downside risks for the dollar against the yen. This is because as Japan continues to recover, U.S. markets are losing their attraction. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Speculative Interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest bout of yen buying was started by speculative interest.\par European dealers guessed that the Bank of Japan hadn't left any yen\par selling orders with the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over Wednesday's Japanese\par bank holiday, and looked to trigger stop-loss selling of dollars and\par euros. Once the dollar broke Y105, investors' hedging requirements took\par over.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Anyone who had any sort of position had to hedge it when the dollar\par went through Y105" Wednesday, Celaya said. "The more it goes the quicker\par it goes, and the more people are forced to get in at wrong levels."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Midmorning in Europe, the dollar hit a low of Y103.20, its weakest in\par 44 months and down from Y105.92 late in New York Tuesday. The euro hit a\par new record low of Y106.90, down from Y109.68 late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although foreign inflows into the Japanese equity market have often\par been cited as the motive force behind yen strength, the recent\par performance of the Nikkei stock index doesn't bear this out. The\par benchmark index fell 0.7% Tuesday and the Tokyo market was closed\par Wednesday, during which two days the dollar tumbled more than three yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In fact, the Nikkei has stalled just below the 18,000 level," says\par Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in New York.\par He points out that data last Friday showed foreigners as net sellers of\par Japanese equities to the tune of Y200 billion in August, as against net\par purchases of Y1.1 trillion in July and Y1.0 trillion in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Hedging Takes Over\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par What has taken over as the main driver of yen strength has been\par hedging of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposures by Japanese holders of euro- and\par dollar-denominated assets, in reaction to the very yen rally which\par hedging thereby perpetuates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Initially, this hedging pressure came primarily against the euro,\par because Japanese investors' average entry price for euro investments was\par further away from current levels than the entry price for dollar\par investments. But as the yen has continued to rally, Japanese investors'\par greater exposure to the dollar may be starting to come to bear.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In research published Wednesday, Deutsche Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economist Paul\par Meggyesi shows that total Japanese purchases of U.S. debt have totaled\par Y13.1 trillion over the past five years, at an average dollar rate of\par Y112.50. While purchases of euro debt were smaller, totaling Y9.1\par trillion, they took place at a more painful average euro rate of\par Y140.10.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It is no wonder that Japanese liquidation of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par exposure has so far been concentrated on the euro," Meggyesi said. "As\par the yen continues to rally, however, more and more Japanese investors\par will begin to show a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loss on their dollar assets."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This could open up fresh downside for the dollar against the yen,\par "even from current levels," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. securities' markets negative reaction to Tuesday's U.S. retail\par sales and current-account deficit releases may also portend more dollar\par woes to come. The benchmark U.S. 30-year bond lost nearly a point\par Tuesday, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 1.1%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par              Asia Recovery Helps\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An improving Japanese economy provides the best argument for further\par and sustained gains in the yen. The latest bout of yen strength was\par triggered by the surprise report earlier this month of a 0.2% rise in\par Japan's second-quarter GDP, following the 2% expansion seen in the first\par quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets will now be looking for confirmation of this improved outlook\par from the next Tankan survey of business sentiment from the Bank of\par Japan, out Oct. 4.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the past, conventional wisdom has been that Japanese officials\par would never allow the yen to continue to rally because of the experience\par of how a surging yen choked off Japan's last economic recovery in 1995.\par But with Asian regional economies now recovering from their 1996-1998\par crisis, that trade-competitiveness argument may no longer hold true.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japan is running a large trade surplus, and growth in many of Japan's\par regional Asian trading partners is now recovering so Japan's exports\par will too," said Tony Norfield, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at\par ABN-AMRO.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has continued to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the\par rising yen, the lack of any systemic threat or of generalized dollar\par weakness leads most observers to believe that the time for U.S. official\par involvement hasn't yet come.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It may come soon, however. Japan's capital and financial account has\par turned around from a deficit of 5% of gross domestic product a year ago\par to a rough balance, reducing the money available to flow into the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Capital outflows from Japan are no longer sufficient to fund the U.S.\par current account deficit," says Robin Aspinall, chief economist in Europe\par for National Australia Bank. "Tremendous pressure is now building on the\par dollar."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The threat, Aspinall warns, is that a weak dollar, weak stocks, and a\par weak U.S. economy could "feed on each other in a downward spiral that\par would destabilize world markets and undermine world growth."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Andrew Joncus; 44-171-842-9494; andrew.joncus@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-09-99