\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Faces Pressure To Leave  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Action Unsterilized-Nikkei \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 02:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei)--A move appears to be building within the Japanese government to rethink the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of sterilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at checking the yen's sharp appreciation, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun (Nikkei) reported in its Wednesday morning edition. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ remains firmly opposed to the idea of conducting unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- leaving yen sold for dollars in the money markets, thus effectively easing monetary policy by expanding the money supply. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing growing political pressure amid the ineffectiveness of its moves to curb the yen's rise in isolation from its U.S. counterpart, the Nikkei reported. \par \par \uc2  Taichi Sakaiya, head of the Economic Planning Agency, touched off the campaign Tuesday with comments suggesting that leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized is a "possible policy option." \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya's remarks followed in the wake of the impact created Sunday when Eisuke Sakakibara, a still influential former \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, issued what amounted to a call for a further easing of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "If the government issues more bonds by compiling a second supplementary budget, the resulting upward pressure on interest rates would cause the yen to strengthen," Sakakibara said in a television appearance Sunday. "What (the BOJ) can do is to ease monetary policy further." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, for its part, insists that it is already supplying more than enough liquidity to the money markets under its near-zero interest rate policy. In fact, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first started to push overnight interest rates toward zero in February, it has kept the cushion of excess reserves in the system at 1 trillion yen or higher. \par \par \uc2  "We are supplying far more liquidity under the zero interest rate policy than we can through unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a BOJ official said. \par \par \uc2  But some market insiders contend that the BOJ's resistance to sterilizing its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is actually acting to underpin the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. and European hedge funds are starting to believe that it is safe to expand their yen buying," says Tetsufumi Yamakawa of Goldman Sachs (Japan) Ltd., "because it is highly unlikely for the BOJ to shift course on its sterilization policy," the Nikkei reported. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa has so far publicly backed the BOJ, applauding the positive impact of its near-zero interest rate policy. But if long-term interest rates -- now at the 1.7% level -- start to show signs of a sharp rise, the political pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certain to intensify. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0013GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9f06rcx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
