\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Official: Keep Current Policy, Supply Ample Funds \b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 18:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Chikako Mogi \par \par \uc2  PARIS (Dow Jones)--A senior Bank of Japan official said Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue its current loose monetary policy and provide ample funds to the Japanese money market, amid the yen's continued rise on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to Dow Jones Newswires, Yutaka Yamaguchi, a deputy BOJ governer, declined to comment on the recent rapid acceleration of the yen's rise against the dollar and the euro. \par \par \uc2  He also declined comment on recent debate in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the BOJ should unsterilize its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, speaking on the sidelines of the International Stability Forum meeting, Yamaguchi said that "in light of all the circumstances, the BOJ will continue to keep the current monetary policy, through which it has provided excessive funds to the market." \par \par \uc2  He was replying to a question about whether the BOJ is concerned that the yen's appreciation will undermine Japan's weak economic recovery, or whether the BOJ would consider leaving the funds in the market after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Earlier, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Minister of Finance for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda declined to comment on the yen's appreciation when questioned by Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide against the yen came to a halt in early New York trading Wednesday, but the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains well bid against its U.S. counterpart and the euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at Y104.23 around 1530 GMT, having hit a 44-month low of Y103.20 around 0901 GMT Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 15-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1610GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9f06rc3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
