\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Mighty yen roars to new peaks vs dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 1,157 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 13:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The yen continued its relentless advance against the dollar and the euro on Wednesday, soaring to new highs on Wednesday in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen hit 103.25 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its highest since January 1996, and an all-time peak of 106.84 against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Lack of action by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a Japanese market holiday encouraged yen bulls to push the dollar lower and the slide was amplified by stop-loss orders as the yen burst one chart barrier after another and neared the psychological threshold of 100 yen per dollar. "We've been looking for dollar/yen to fall to 100 for a while. Today's slide is just a continuation of the recent move," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Sterns in London. \par \par \uc2  "(Japanese Finance Minister) Miyazawa said on Tuesday there is no holiday for the Bank of Japan but the market clearly does not think that." \par \par \uc2  By 1040 GMT dollar/yen was at 103.69/79 compared with 105.25/30 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had shed up to more than 2.5 percent on the day and nearly eight yen since the release of Japan's surprise gross domestic product data on September 9 which showed growth in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was at 107.59/69 compared with 109.64/68 in late European trading on Tuesday. The euro has fallen more than 10 yen since September 9. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen needed a co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse its bullish course, but so far both the Japanese and the U.S. authorities had denied the yen would be at the centre of discussions of a G7 meeting on September 25. \par \par \uc2  Along this line, Japanese Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano told the French newspaper, Les Echos, he did not expect "a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major central banks". \par \par \uc2  "I would not raise any hope that there would be any action to stem the yen rise at the G7 other than words," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Julius Baer Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0371/75 against a day's low of $1.0307 and compared with $1.0417/24 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The single European unit had failed to capitalise decisively on broad dollar weakness and remained sidelined for most of the European morning session as dealers awaited for the release of key U.S. August CPI data at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Barrow at Bear Stearns said the fundamental macroeconomic scenario for the euro zone had not changed and expected the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to regain vigour in the medium-term. \par \par \uc2  "The euro does not deserve the fate that has befallen it since it got down from (its August peak) above $1.08," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. CPI figures would be crucial in determining prospects for a further hike in U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expected on average August U.S. consumer prices to rise an adjusted 0.3 percent. Ex-food and energy prices were expected to increase by 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Howard at Julius Baer said a tame number could bring relief to the bruised U.S. asset markets and spur a brief dollar rally. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling got a boost from stronger-than-expected British labour market data, which seemed to confirm the rising trend for higher UK rates, but the rally was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "There's really not much to get aggressively bullish on sterling because the interest rate outlook had not really changed," said Adian Schmidt, international economist at Chase Investment Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England hiked its key repo rate to 5.25 percent last week. The pound was at $1.0640/50 against a day's high of $1.0699. \par \par \uc2  By 1040 GMT dollar/yen was at 103.69/79 compared with 105.25/30 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had shed up to more than 2.5 percent on the day and nearly eight yen since the release of Japan's surprise gross domestic product data on September 9 which showed growth in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was at 107.59/69 compared with 109.64/68 in late European trading on Tuesday. The euro has fallen more than 10 yen since September 9. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen needed a co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse its bullish course, but so far both the Japanese and the U.S. authorities had denied the yen would be at the centre of discussions of a G7 meeting on September 25. \par \par \uc2  Along this line, Japanese Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano told the French newspaper, Les Echos, he did not expect "a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major central banks". \par \par \uc2  "I would not raise any hope that there would be any action to stem the yen rise at the G7 other than words," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Julius Baer Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0371/75 against a day's low of $1.0307 and compared with $1.0417/24 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The single European unit had failed to capitalise decisively on broad dollar weakness and remained sidelined for most of the European morning session as dealers awaited for the release of key U.S. August CPI data at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Barrow at Bear Stearns said the fundamental macroeconomic scenario for the euro zone had not changed and expected the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to regain vigour in the medium-term. \par \par \uc2  "The euro does not deserve the fate that has befallen it since it got down from (its August peak) above $1.08," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. CPI figures would be crucial in determining prospects for a further hike in U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expected on average August U.S. consumer prices to rise an adjusted 0.3 percent. Ex-food and energy prices were expected to increase by 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Howard at Julius Baer said a tame number could bring relief to the bruised U.S. asset markets and spur a brief dollar rally. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling got a boost from stronger-than-expected British labour market data, which seemed to confirm the rising trend for higher UK rates, but the rally was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "There's really not much to get aggressively bullish on sterling because the interest rate outlook had not really changed," said Adian Schmidt, international economist at Chase Investment Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England hiked its key repo rate to 5.25 percent last week. The pound was at $1.0640/50 against a day's high of $1.0699. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9f00kiq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
