\par\par \b \uc2  Yen suffers bout of vertigo after breakneck climb. \b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 01:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The yen suffered a brief bout of vertigo on Thursday, backing away from the stratospheric heights reached against the dollar and euro overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said many in the market were rushing to cover short positions in case the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  protested the speed of the yen's climb by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . None, however, thought \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would turn the trend. \par \par \uc2  "They need the U.S. to come in or to adopt unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a U.S. bank. "But the U.S. doesn't seem concerned and the BOJ is all for sterilising, so the yen will keep rising until it's ridiculously expensive." \par \par \uc2  By 2315 GMT the dollar had bounced to 105.14/19 yen from a deep low around 103.20/25 overnight, the lowest reading since January 1996. \par \par \uc2  But it was still well adrift of the 106.55 level it held late here on Tuesday before the Tokyo holiday on Wednesday and more than five percent down from the 111.10 seen before last week's surprisingly strong Japanese GDP data triggered this latest selloff. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also clawing back some losses to stand at 109.28/26 yen from a fresh lifetime low around 106.84 overnight, but again was short of Tuesday's late 110.32. Against the dollar the euro had returned to $1.0400/02 after it briefly dipped as low as $1.0287. \par \par \uc2  Dealers feared the sheer speed of the yen's rise meant Japanese authorities would have no option but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if they knew it had little chance of turning the trend. The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last on Tuesday, to little effect, but Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said then that if movements were too rapid "there's no choice but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also wary of a report in the Yomiuri Shimbun newspaper claiming Japanese monetary authorities have started discussing possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their U.S. and European counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The paper cites an unnamed international monetary source saying the yen's move to 103.00 was "excessive." \par \par \uc2  However, dealers and analysts were not convinced the United States was yet ready to join \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They noted Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers merely repeated the standard line that a strong dollar is very much in the national interest of the United States. \par \par \uc2  While Federal Reserve Board Governor Edward Gramlich said the U.S. dollar was stable, despite its recent slide against the Japanese yen. "The overall dollar is pretty stable. It is weakening against some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and going up against others," Gramlich told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts often note that measured against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the United States major trading partners the dollar is still close to its highs for the last decade. \par \par \uc2  And the European authorities appear even more sanguine on the weakness of the euro, with recent comments highlighting the benefits for exports of a low \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa all but admitted as much on Tuesday when he said the strong yen was Japan's problem and was unlikely to be discussed at the forthcoming G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Another option would be for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop sterilising its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thereby leaving more yen in the markets. Theoretically that would expand the money supply and push money rates lower, so weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, money rates are already close to zero and the makret is already flooded with liquidity, with the BOJ leaving a trillion yen surplus in the market every day. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ itself has argued that unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not work since there is already plenty of liquidity in the market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant because if unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did indeed fail, it would then be under intense pressure to take much more dramatic steps such as adopting an inflation target or monetising the government's debt by printing money to buy JGBs. \par \par \uc2  An indication of the stance will come Friday when spot contracts from Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are settled. If the bank draws off the extra liquidity, as most expect, it will be a clear sign of its opposition to unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9f00jym\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
