\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Usui -2: Speaking To Reporters \b0\par\par\uc2 128 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 03:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Japan's administrative vice finance minister reiterated Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains opposed to rapid movements in the yen and that it continues to watch markets closely. \par \par \uc2  Nobuaki Usui, speaking to reporters upon entering the ministry, declined to comment on the dollar-yen's fall overnight to a 40-month low of Y106.10, except to say that, "sudden yen strengthening is undesirable and the government's stance towards \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unchanged." \par \par \uc2  When asked whether he thought joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary to combat the steep rise in the yen, he said, "(The ministry) is watching markets cautiously and will take appropriate steps as needed." \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0109GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9e06rc4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
