\par\par \b \uc2  Deutsche Bank's Courtis: BOJ Policy Penalizes Japan Cos. \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 19:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--As long as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to "sterilize" its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy will suffer downward pressure even though fiscal stimulus there is achieving real results, Deutsche Bank Asia Pacific Economist Kenneth Courtis said in a conference call last week. \par \par \uc2  Courtis said he sees a severe clash between the expansionary views of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets fiscal policy, and the BOJ, which carries it out and sets monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Japanese companies will be "severely penalized as a result of these policies," Courtis said, adding that the BOJ's decision to sterilize \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a mistake on par with the MOF's decision in 1996 to raise taxes. \par \par \uc2  "If you want to see the yen drive from Y122.00 to Y105.00, where it is this morning, you do exactly what the Bank of Japan is doing," Courtis said, of the dollar's fall and the yen's gain over the last two months. \par \par \uc2  At 12:45 p.m. EDT (1645 GMT), the dollar was worth Y105.56, down from Y106.60 late Monday despite another round of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  In a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting in June, estimated at a total of $41 billion, the BOJ has bought dollars for yen to weaken the Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it has consistently sterilized its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mopping up excess liquidity via money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Eventually, protests from Japanese exporters and the MOF "will force the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse course and embrace unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Courtis said. \par \par \uc2  The resulting flood of liquidity would force the yen to beat at retreat to the Y120.00s and Y130.00s, Courtis said, adding that U.S. finance officials would be more willing to support unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's too-tight monetary policy will force a correction in Japanese stocks - a correction which should be viewed as a buying opportunity, Courtis said. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the yen could prove a boon for the rest of Asia as well, Courtis added. "It will encourage more exports from those countries to Japan." \par \par \uc2  And a dollar/yen rate at Y105.00, "takes enormous pressure off China to devalue" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, Courtis said. He added that he doesn't think the question of a Chinese devaluation will become an issue for the markets "until well into next year." \par \par \uc2  But once the BOJ loosens the reins, Japan's economy has room to grow further on the back of generous governmental spending, he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has committed an estimated $1 trillion in U.S. dollars in the past 12 months to provide fiscal stimulus, help small companies, get banks to restructure and fund unemployment supports - a plan that is "New Deal in dimension," Courtis said. Already, Japan has surprised market watchers with two consecutive quarters of growth this year, he noted. \par \par \uc2  On the whole, Courtis expects all of Asia to grow 15% more this year than it did in 1998, with steady growth across all Asian countries except for Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  All that growth will prop up commodities prices, he said. But lingering as a negative are the high debt loads carried across Asia, he said. \par \par \uc2  For the U.S., Courtis anticipates the Federal Reserve will raise short term rates three more times by the end of the first quarter of next year at 25 basis points each time, which should take some of the froth out of the economy but not be enough to thwart growing price pressures. \par \par \uc2  "The long term equation is too powerful for prices not to go higher," he said. \par \par \uc2  All in all, Courtis expects Asian markets outside Japan to continue to outperform markets in North America this year. \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 23-09-99) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1730GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9e06qrp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
