\par\par \b \uc2  Cenbank source sees no ECB role for now on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 11:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PARIS, Sept 14 (Reuters) - A source from one European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday he did not think the ECB needed to help Japan contain the yen's recent surge. \par \par \uc2  The source, from the economics department of one European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was attending a "routine meeting" of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry officials at the Paris-based Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development (OECD). \par \par \uc2  "I don't think so," the source replied when asked if he believed the ECB had a role to play in controlling the yen's recent surge against the dollar and euro. "It's also a matter of degree, basically. At least I'm not worried about the current situation." \par \par \uc2  The source said the theory behind this analysis of the situation was that fluctuations between the dollar, euro and yen were natural now that fluctuations within the euro zone had been eliminated by the creation of the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "That's a fact of life," he said. \par \par \uc2  He made it clear that there was also a question of degree. \par \par \uc2  "If it really gets out of hand something has to be done," he said. \par \par \uc2  The source was attending a regular meeting of the OECD which discusses the global economic situation. Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials from all the main economic powers attend these meetings under the auspices of the 29-nation OECD. \par \par \uc2  "The agenda is quite routine," the source said in reference to the generally secretive discussions at OECD headquarters. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at 106.35 to the dollar and 110.10 to the euro on Tuesday. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was estimated to have spent about $2 billion in a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar on Friday. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese government spokesman confirmed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday and said the government would continue to take decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. ((Brian Love, Paris newsroom +33 1 4221 5452; paris.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9e00o42\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
