\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips again as market scoffs at BOJ action. \b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 09:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The dollar was once more on the slide against the yen by late afternoon Tokyo on Tuesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to deter yen bulls for long, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 106.17/24 yen at 0620 GMT, compared with 106.64/72 yen in New York late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback slipped as low as 106 yen in early trade, the lowest level since May 1996, before the BOJ stepped into the market in late morning to buy dollars and lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as high as 107.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overwhelmed by aggressive yen buying led by U.S. fund operators, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As was the case last Friday, the BOJ action was probably not intended to push the dollar/yen rate significantly higher, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not strong enough to change the market's sentiment. So a test of 105 yen (to the dollar) seems to be only a matter of time," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  Bankers estimated that Tuesday's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably did not exceed $1.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  They had estimated that the BOJ spent about $2 billion last Friday when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise for the first time since July 21, much less than in its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June and July. \par \par \uc2  Despite the efforts of the Japanese authorities, who are concerned that a strong yen may derail the economy's nascent recovery, the dollar has now fallen by about 15 percent against the yen from this year's high of 124.75 hit on May 20. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui said on Tuesday that Japan's monetary authorities took action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in line with remarks by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said earlier that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there are excessive movements. \par \par \uc2  "Usui's confirmation may have been counterproductive for financial authorities," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "It gave the market the impression that the authorities are quite powerless in halting the yen's rise," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said weak Tokyo stocks may have given authorities an additional incentive to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The key Nikkei 225 average ended down 132.07 points or 0.74 percent at 17,777.22, after briefly falling as low as 17,610.31. \par \par \uc2  "A fall in the share prices of Japanese exporters may have caused Japanese authorities to become worried about the impact on share prices of the yen's rise to this year's high," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The market was unmoved by late afternoon comments by top government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka saying that Japan will continue taking decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was briefly lifted against the yen after late morning by comments by Japan's Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya that monetary policy measures may be included in the government's next economic package. \par \par \uc2  "There were several dollar-supportive factors, such as lower Tokyo stock prices, lower Japanese government bond yields and hints of possible monetary easing by Sakaiya," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  "But yen-buying interest from foreign players bullish about Japan's economic prospects simply outweighed those yen negative factors," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Position squaring by Tokyo players ahead of a Japanese market holiday on Wednesday briefly helped lift the dollar near 107 yen in mid-afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Some players suspected that the BOJ might have been behind those gains. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0351/56 at 0705 GMT, down from $1.0421/29 in New York late on Monday. ((Kanta Watanabe, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9e00n7z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
