\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr, Euro Dn Vs Yen, As BOJ Nowhere In Sight \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 07:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan was a no-show in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in Asia Monday, which gave traders a chance to sell both dollars and euros for yen, pushing the euro to a new low against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Market players took advantage of the BOJ's absence to sell dollar-yen and euro-yen for various purposes," a dealer at a Japanese trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that Japanese exporters and investors sold dollar-yen for hedging, while U.S. funds sold dollar-yen and European players sold euro-yen to invest in Japanese equities. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a new low of Y111.97 during the session on heavy selling by European players eager to purchase Japanese equities. There was also some selling by Japanese life insurers looking to hedge or cut losses on euro-denominated assets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was absent as the dollar fell below Y109 Monday, although it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday to drive it close to Y110. But market players are still somewhat cautious about pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, on lingering fears that the BOJ might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prop up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar is likely to stay in a range of Y107.80-Y108.80 for the rest of the Asian session," said a Tokyo-based European bank dealer. "The dollar's upside is capped by heavy offers from Japanese exporters and investors as well as U.S. funds, while the dollar's downside is supported by fears of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks increasingly unlikely following comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers last Friday. Summers reiterated that the strong dollar is very much in the interest of the U.S., but he also said, "What's right is not to seek to manage the markets, but instead to concentrate on building the right fundamentals for our economy." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that these remarks seem to imply that there is very little possibility that the U.S. would cooperate with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a view reinforced Monday by comments made by Keio University professor Makoto Utsumi, a former vice minister for international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets) for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sake," Utsumi said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y108.16, below Y108.75 late Friday in New York and down from Y108.78 late Friday in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0360, lower than $1.0372 late Friday in New York but above $1.0395 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y112.05, down from Y112.80 in New York late Friday and below Y113.08 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6212, up from $1.6190 in New York late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6180 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 13-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0535GMT \par \par