\par\par \b \uc2  Yen powers higher in early U.S., more upside seen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 16:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen showed no sign of slowing down in early U.S. trade on Monday after racing overnight to lifetime peaks against the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and three-year highs versus the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Optimism about Japan's economic recovery was driving the yen higher and the absence of fresh Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply reinforced the market's enthusiasm for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The yen has appreciated dramatically overnight and I think it should continue," said Dennis Heidt, chief dealer at Banque Paribas. "It looks like 103 (yen per dollar) is within reach in a reasonable amount of time." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a low of 106.05 yen in early U.S. trade, slicing through technical support levels with little effort, to shed nearly three percent from its overnight high above 109 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro also crumbled to an all-time low of 109.58 yen, down some three percent from the session's high of 112.90, before staging a modest bounce back above 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they remained on alert for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese monetary authorities to prop up the dollar or euro against the surging yen. \par \par \uc2  But concerns have diminished since a bout of BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday had no lasting effect on the yen-hungry market. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said overnight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to "sterilize" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by draining excess liquidity from the private sector banking system resulting from selling yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers say future \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action is unlikely to have any lasting effect unless Japanese authorities leave an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilized," seen as a de facto credit easing. \par \par \uc2  "The market may just have to find its own bottom, but who knows where that will be," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader John Cholakis at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank. He pegged 105.70 yen as a key technical support level for the dollar, but said it was unlikely to be more than a "speed bump" in the yen's ride higher. \par \par \uc2  The world's top central bankers, meeting in Switzerland, offered the yen even more impetus to soar as U.S. trade got underway. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Eddie George, speaking to reporters after a meeting of Group of 10 and other central bankers in Switzerland, said there was a general understanding for last week's Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "given the size and speed of the movement in the yen." \par \par \uc2  George also said that the Japanese economy was growing significantly faster than had been thought only six months ago: "...I think everyone shared the view that the prospect for sustained growth in Japan is better than it has been." \par \par \uc2  Separately, the Bank of England declined to comment on whether it had bought dollars against yen on behalf of the BoJ during Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tokyo banking sources said earlier the BoE had been detected acting on behalf of Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar gave up overnight gains against the euro after the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had dipped to near two-month lows just below $1.03 overnight, traded back to session highs around $1.0390 after U.S. trading got under way. \par \par \uc2  The euro had been hurt overnight by a setback for German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's Social Democrats in Sunday's regional elections, which was expected to make it more difficult to pass reforms. \par \par \uc2  "Today we might have a bit of a pullback in the dollar against the euro," said Heidt. "But we look like we've seen a resumption of a dollar uptrend against Europe and that's a trend that should continue with some quasi interruptions here and there." \par \par \uc2  The British pound extended Friday's decline from last week's rally to four-month highs against the dollar, which had followed a surprise rate hike by the Bank of England. It was trading near the session low of $1.6108 in early U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  ((-North American Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1868)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00uo8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
