\par\par \b \uc2  Yen continues relentless climb. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 13:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The yen scaled all-time highs against the euro and three-year peaks against the dollar on Monday, buoyed by optimism over Japan's economy and unperturbed by the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  An absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged yen bulls to keep pushing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, particularly after Friday's solo Bank of Japan dollar-buying had negligible impact on the yen's relentless upward momentum, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's still a story of capital flows continuing to outweigh anything that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do," said Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. "The price action from Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been extremely disappointing for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The Euro was trading at 109.95/110.06 yen at 1143 GMT, compared with 113.60/79 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 106.40/45. \par \par \uc2  The dollar showed little resilience on its way down, with the drops through 107.50 yen and 107.00 yen triggering flurries of stop-loss dollar sale orders in textbook fashion and accelerating the decline. \par \par \uc2  "There's really nothing to stop this move right now, and we have just been taking out stops after stops," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Short of concerted international \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said a de-facto BOJ credit easing through so-called unsterlised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be the only card the Japanese authorities had at the moment to halt the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  However, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said overnight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  planned to keep sterilising its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - meaning it would drain excess liquidity in the private sector banking system resulting from its dollar buying for yen. \par \par \uc2  While most traders were sceptical that any coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent, they said the market could get jumpy should the dollar keep rising ahead of the September 25 meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yen's broad strength was driven primarily by its demand against the euro, which was broadly weaker after German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's Social Democrats suffered another defeat in Sunday's regional elections, a setback for the government's effort to push through its austerity programme. \par \par \uc2  The preliminary second quarter gross domestic product for the 11 euro member countries also came in slightly below expectations, adding insult to injury. \par \par \uc2  The Q2 GDP was up 0.3 percent on the quarter, compared with economists' average forecast of a 0.4-percent gain. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was last trading at $1.0339/43 against $1.0445/50 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Still, Lewis at Deutsche said the euro's losses against the dollar could be limited in the short term, given the narrowing yield spreads between U.S. Treasuries and German bunds which made holding the euro-asset more attractive relatively. \par \par \uc2  The yield spread between 10-year T-notes and bunds have tightened to 90 basis points. The spread last closed at that level in December 1998. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a very good predictor in the past," Lewis said of the spread. "In the past three, four weeks, there has been a divergence between what the interest rate market is saying and what the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is saying." \par \par \uc2  He added that a similar divergence had occurred in June/July, when the euro bottomed at alltime lows near $1.01 and subsequently rallied above $1.08. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think we're heading for parity ($1.00) just yet," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00unw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
