\par\par \b \uc2  Yen buying on Japan optimism drags down euro, dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The euro struggled near its life low against the yen by late Monday afternoon due to increasing optimism about the outlook for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  As of 0610 GMT, the euro's record low was 111.95 yen, set earlier in the day in Tokyo, spurred by selling by U.S. banks and Japanese investors, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  That was down over 17 percent from its record high of 135.10 set on January 4. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also faltered against the yen due to a rise in Tokyo share prices and some selling by Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 108.20/25 yen as of 0611 GMT against 108.69/79 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 112.20/39 yen as of 0615 GMT against 112.74 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0372/77 against $1.0363/73 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar and euro were both supported by lingering wariness about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  They said they were mindful of the fact the BOJ began its dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday at levels just below 108 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are probably well aware of the existence of stop-loss dollar offers below 108.00 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen levels near 108.00 yen are still sensitive, due to the existence of stop-loss dollar offers below that level," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  It is conceivable that Japanese authorities may conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to prevent such offers from being triggered and accelerating rises in the yen, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But others said wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had eased somewhat due to the firmness of Tokyo share prices. \par \par \uc2  In the stock market, the key Nikkei 225 average closed at 17,909.29, up 1.12 percent or 198.27 points from Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was limited impact from comments by former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara on a Sunday television programme. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, currently a professor at Tokyo's Keio University, said the BOJ should cooperate with MOF to stop an excessive rise in the yen through a further easing of its monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has said it will not ease its monetary policy further and the market thinks the yen's rise is acceptable to the BOJ," Sakakibara said in the TV programme. \par \par \uc2  A dealer for another city bank said: "The market did not pay much attention because he is no longer a MOF official." \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui played down the remarks, saying Sakakibara's views were his own and did not reflect the ministry's stance. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said late on Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to continue sterilising \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by draining excess yen funds from the Tokyo money market. \par \par \uc2  Settlement of BOJ's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday will take place Tuesday and there was some speculation the BOJ might leave the excess funds in the market in order to conduct a further "quantitative easing" of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Bankers estimated the BOJ spent approximately $2 billion on Friday to prop up the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also told a regular news conference that recent movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market have been a bit rapid and said the BOJ will carefully watch the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements on the economy. \par \par \uc2  He said \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible when movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are too rapid. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said in an interview in New Zealand that while an excessive rise in the yen was undesirable for Japan's economy, it would aid Asian economies by spurring exports to Japan. Obuchi added that the yen rise partly reflects confidence in Japan. \par \par \uc2  The second city bank dealer said government and BOJ officials seemed to be taking a relatively relaxed stance towards recent rises in the yen now that the government's growth target of 0.5 percent seemed achievable. "In such an environment, I think monetary authorities may conduct smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at slowing the yen's ascent but I don't think they feel a need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change the market trend," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  After last Thursday's announcement that Japan's April-June GDP rose 0.2 percent from the previous quarter or 0.9 percent on an annualised basis, Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya said the 0.5 percent growth target "is certain to be acheived". ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00tkn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
