\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro stays on downtrend, sets lows vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 06:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The euro stayed on a downtrend versus the yen in Tokyo on Monday due to differences in growth of the two economies and European officials' reluctance to allow the euro to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  It set new all-time lows by midday. \par \par \uc2  Some Japanese investors were detected selling euros for yen ahead of Japanese firms' bookclosings for the first half on September 30, weighing further on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was traded at 112.35/46 yen at 0348 GMT, after hitting a fresh record low of 112.35 yen earlier on Monday morning. The euro closed New York at 112.74 yen on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0374/84, little changed from $1.0363/73 in New York late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There was no single aggressive player to blame for the euro's fall against the yen," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  But generally speaking, Japanese investors were adjusting their foreign asset positions ahead of book closings, providing support to the yen against the euro as well as against the dollar, he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 108.22/27 yen at 0349 GMT, compared with 108.69/79 yen in New York late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said since the euro broke below a technical support of 112.46 yen, equivalent to the deutschemark's all-time low of 57.50 yen, charts suggests it could fall towards 110 yen and below. \par \par \uc2  But some said the market is becoming cautious about selling the euro too aggressively as that could invite the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ stepped in to support the dollar against the yen last Friday and spent approximately $2 billion, bankers estimated, in a move aimed at keeping the Japanese economy's fragile recovery from stalling. \par \par \uc2  In a monthly report released on Monday, the BOJ maintained its cautious assessment of the nation's economy. \par \par \uc2  The report said exports and industrial output had turned upward over the past month but the strong yen was biting into corporate profits, while housing investment, the brightest star in last week's economic data, was headed for a downturn. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors would detest to see the euro falling to 110 yen and below. That would mean the euro's depreciation by 15 percent from the level of the previous book closing at the end of March," said a senior trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under pressure especially since last week, when key economic data showed a gap in the speed of recovery between Japanese and European economies. \par \par \uc2  Another problem for the euro was a lack of will by European officials to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The latest such comments came from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Eugenio Domingo Solans on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We want a stable euro, not necessarily a strong euro. In the long term the euro will derive strength from its stability," Solans said in a speech at an investment seminar in Denmark. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government said last Thursday that the economy grew a modest 0.2 percent in the April-June quarter from the previous quarter, after posting stunning growth of 2.0 percent in the first three months of the year. \par \par \uc2  That made a sharp contrast with stagnant growth data announced in Germany, the biggest economy in the euro region. \par \par \uc2  The German Federal Statistics Office said last Thursday the nation's GDP was unchanged from the previous quarter, when it had grown 0.4 percent from the final quarter of 1998. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00ss0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
