\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. T-bond little changed in Tokyo, eye dlr/yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 05:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 13 (Reuters) - Key 30-year U.S. Treasury bond was little changed by midday Tokyo on Monday, after rallying in New York on Friday on tame August core U.S. producer price index, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment grew slightly bearish as the dollar deteriorated against the yen. The dollar was quoted at 108.48/53 yen at 0210 GMT, compared with 109.12/15 yen in early Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  Market expected to be rangebound ahead of key August U.S. consumer price index to be released on Wednesday. Strong data would raise speculation of another U.S. credit tightening by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  Bellwether bond was unchanged at 101-5/32, yielding 6.04 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There is little movement this morning... the market is very thin," a trader at a Japanese brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  The core producer price index (PPI), which excludes food and energy costs, fell 0.1 percent in August compared to Wall Street expectations of a 0.1 percent increase. \par \par \uc2  Rising costs for oil and crops hurt by the summer's drought sent the overall PPI up 0.5 percent, more than the 0.3 percent gain the market had expected. \par \par \uc2  A city bank trader said: "Bond prices failed to rise further as the dollar faltered on expectations of no joint action to stem the yen's rise." \par \par \uc2  "Key factors this week will be U.S. data and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," the bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect, on average, August's CPI to show a 0.3 percent increase and a 0.2 percent increase in the core CPI, which excludes food and energy prices. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they would be wary of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a Japanese national holiday on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "No one wants to be caught short before the holiday as the BOJ could ask the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf," another city bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  The Fed last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on the BOJ's behalf on July 20, a Japanese national holiday. \par \par \uc2  But many traders are resigned to a yen-bullish trend, seeing Japan's actions as a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper the dollar's falls rather than to reverse its course. \par \par \uc2  "Yen-based investors expect the dollar to weaken further. So investors such as life insurers are upping their hedging ratios against dollar-based investments," the brokerage trader said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, net foreign bond investment by Japanese investors in August totalled 1.0677 trillion yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data showed on Friday. \par \par \uc2  MOF said net foreign bond buying by private life insurers, financial institutions and investment trusts amounted to only 517.8 billion yen, leaving nearly 550 billion yen worth of net foreign bond buying unaccounted for. \par \par \uc2  The unaccounted-for portion is made up mostly of foreign bonds purchased via brokerage firms, according to MOF. \par \par \uc2  The brokerages could have been buying for their own accounts, as well as for retail investors, non-financial corporations and public funds. \par \par \uc2  Analysts suspect public funds accounted for a large percentage because of the large size of the sum. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to imagine that retail investors are purchasing such a large amount of foreign bonds. Most retail money is going into Japanese stocks, anyway," the brokerage trader said. \par \par \uc2  MOF data on Monday showed net foreign bond buying by Japanese based on settlements totalled 605.4 billion yen in July. \par \par \uc2  The key December T-bond futures contract was at 113-27/32, down 4/32 from the previous close. It traded in a range of 113-26/32 to 113/30/32. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00spd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
