\par\par \b \uc2  Euro regains footing, yen sidelined at US midday. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 19:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 13 (Reuters) - Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the dollar and pared losses against the Japanese yen in midday trading on Monday, winning a reprieve from recent declines as the greenback remained heavy. \par \par \uc2  "The market has overshot, the way the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market often does, and we're seeing people lock in some profits," said Ben Strauss, vice president at Bank Julius Baer, explaining the euro's modest comeback. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held off making new lows against the yen after hitting a three-year trough at 106.05 in early U.S. trade, but dealers said it remains vulnerable to further yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The euro swung back above $1.04, up more than a full cent from a two-month low it hit in overnight trading, when it slid 3 percent below the peaks it hit last week against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The European unit also rebounded more than a full yen from all-time lows hit earlier below the psychological 110 yen level, and reversed recent declines against the British pound. \par \par \uc2  By contrast a sluggish start for U.S. stocks dragged on an already heavy dollar, traders said. The Dow Jones industrial average was nearly flat in noontime U.S. trade, while the tech-laden Nasdaq softened after Friday's record close. \par \par \uc2  Battered by a steep selloff, the U.S. unit had shed nearly 3 percent from its overnight high above 109 yen as optimism about Japan's economic recovery and the absence of fresh Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  egged on yen bulls. \par \par \uc2  "(The dollar) really is not looking very healthy. There's been selling of dollars across the board today and it appears that's going to continue," said Brian Zirlin, vice president at Donaldson, Lufkin & Jenrette. \par \par \uc2  "We could see 105.50 yen before we see any bounce back up," Zirlin said, adding that a break of the 106 level could trigger stop-loss dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  At 1300 EDT/1700 GMT, the dollar hovered at 106.39/47 yen, down sharply from 108.69/79 in late U.S. Friday trading. The euro stood at $1.0404/12 up from $1.0363/73 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro also put on the brakes against the Japanese yen, climbing above 110.50 yen from a lifetime low of 109.58 yen hit overnight. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they remained on alert for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese monetary authorities to prop up the dollar or euro against the surging yen. \par \par \uc2  But concerns have diminished since a bout of BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday had no lasting effect on the yen-hungry market. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said overnight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to "sterilize" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by draining excess liquidity in the private sector banking system resulting from dollar buying for yen. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers say future \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action is unlikely to have any lasting effect unless Japanese authorities leave an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterlized", seen as a de facto credit easing. \par \par \uc2  "The market may just have to find its own bottom, but who knows where that will be," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader John Cholakis atDai-Ichi Kangyo Bank. He pegged 105.70 yen as a key technical support level for the dollar, but said it was unlikely to be more than a "speed bump" in the yen's ride higher. \par \par \uc2  The world's top central bankers, meeting in Switzerland, offered the yen even more impetus to soar. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Eddie George, speaking after a meeting of Group of 10 and other central bankers in Switzerland, said there was a general understanding for last week's Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and added that the Japanese economy was growing significantly faster than had been thought only six months ago. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound lost a full cent of its value against the dollar, falling to $1.6079/89 from $1.6183/88 in late Friday dealing. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00pq3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
