\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Yen pummels dollar, euro in relentless rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 00:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In first paragraph, please read "The dollar and euro took a beating on Monday ..." instead of "...on Thursday..." (Corrects day of week) \par \par \uc2  (Recasts with latest prices, fresh quotes, new byline) \par \par \uc2  By Daniel Sternoff \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The dollar and euro took a beating on Monday, tumbling nearly 3 percent each against the surging yen, as optimism over Japan's economic prospects fuelled investors' appetites for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On its slide to a lifetime low against the yen, the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also slumped to an eight-week trough against the dollar before trimming some losses against both units. \par \par \uc2  "The market may just have to find its own bottom, but who knows where that will be?" said John Cholakis, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank, of the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The yen shunted the euro below the 110 mark for the first time in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's history to 109.58 yen per euro, and muscled the dollar to a three-year low of 106.05 yen per dollar early in the New York session. \par \par \uc2  The euro spent the rest of the day trying to claw back above 111 to trade 111.06/14 late Monday, still shy of previous lifetime lows of 112 hit at the end of last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also bounced back a bit but was pinned near 106.60 yen for much of the afternoon. The dollar traded at 106.62/67 yen late Monday, down from 108.69/79 yen late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said institutional investors were starting to raise the weight of Japanese assets in their portfolios amid growing sentiment that Japan's economic slump may be bottoming out. \par \par \uc2  Yen bulls were also undeterred by Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably wasting their money unless there's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Europeans and the U.S.," said Charles Malone, chief dealer at Erste Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  "But the U.S. doesn't seem too concerend about the weaker dollar against the yen so I don't think we're going to see any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  While traders saw the dollar continuing to lose ground to the yen in the medium term, they predicted the greenback would make a fresh drive to push the euro towards parity with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think when we moved from near parity (in July) up around $1.08 that it was merited. It was just a correction," Malone said. \par \par \uc2  "We are growing soundly and healthily while in Europe there's still some problems they'll have to overcome.... I have been a believer for a long time that we are going to test at least parity and probably going to test lower," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said losses by German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's Social Democrats in state elections augured ill for German fiscal austerity measures and added to investor uncertainty over Europe's economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  The euro dipped below $1.03 for the first time since it flirted with dollar parity in July, then bounced back up to trade at $1.0422/24 late in the day. \par \par \uc2  "The market has overshot, the way the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market often does, and we're seeing people lock in some profits," said Ben Strauss, vice president at Bank Julius Baer, explaining the euro's modest comeback. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a sluggish day in U.S. stock markets, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average ekeing out a meagre 1.90 points, had held back the dollar, but that sentiment was largely against the euro. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound lost a full cent of its value against the dollar, falling to $1.6075/85 from $1.6183/88 in late Friday dealing. \par \par \uc2  The Candian dollar hit a nine-week high, trading at C$1.4685/95 late on Monday, against C$1.4743/53 late Friday, as Canada buyers took advantage of the U.S. dollar's plunge against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc weakened to 1.5402/12 on Monday against Friday's levels near 1.5509/19 and the Australian dollar rallied to 0.6564/71 against 0.6507/17 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9d00nz2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
