\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr up amid BOJ caution, euro/yen off fresh low. \b0\par\par\uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1999\par 01:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was slightly higher against the yen in early Asia on Monday, supported by expectations that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for the dollar after having stepped into the market last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was off its lows after earlier setting a fresh lifetime low of 112.65 yen in Asia. The previous record low was 112.68 yen set in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 108.91/96 yen at 2310 GMT, compared with 108.69/79 yen in New York late on Friday. The euro was at 112.88/99 yen, compared with 112.74 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I think there is a good chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again today. But that probably would not change the yen's upward trend," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen was supported by increasing optimism about the Japanese economy after the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) for the April-June quarter released last Thursday showed growth for the second consecutive quarter. \par \par \uc2  Bankers estimated the BOJ spent approximately $2 billion last Friday to prop up the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The amount was much less than in its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen in June and July, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  "Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not that aggressive, so the dollar probably will not rise much above 110 yen even if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  again this week," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Former top Japanese financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said on Sunday he thinks United States does not want the dollar to weaken further and that he believes U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is willing to cooperate with Japan. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's remarks followed Friday's comments by Summers saying he was not prepared to discuss "any possible plans or intentions we might have with respect to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  "I don't think we can totally rule out the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But weekend comments by Sakakibara suggest that probably will not happen unless the dollar faces the danger of slipping below 100 yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said on Friday the United States does not want a decline in the dollar below 100 yen as worries about a possible fall in U.S. share prices persist. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0362/72 at 2340 GMT, steady from $1.0363/73 in New York late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback gained strongly against the euro on Friday after the release of benign U.S. producer prices data. \par \par \uc2  The producer price index rose 0.5 percent in August. But the core rate excluding food and energy contracted 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami is scheduled to hold a news conference at 0600 GMT. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9c00tol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
