\par\par \b \uc2  =BOJ Versus Market: Can  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Serve Any Purpose? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marianne Sullivan \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 20:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--By midday Friday in New York, market players were already brushing off \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest attempt to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  More than one was asking why the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  even bothered. Others set their sights on a retest of the dollar's three-year low of Y107.55 hit Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The market wasn't impressed," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York of the Bank of Japan attempt Friday to weaken the yen through the purchase of some $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  The bank, which acts on the order of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hasn't had much success in its efforts to weaken the yen. Its seven previous efforts in June and July that had at best a fleeting effect on the yen's appreciation against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "When it does it like that, it seems self-defeating," said Henry Willmore, chief U.S. economist at Barclays, adding that an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that doesn't spark fear will embolden market players to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the opposite direction. \par \par \uc2  Japan's ability to influence the direction of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deteriorated further Friday when the country's finance minister seemed to rule out the possibility of any coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Kiichi Miyazawa Friday said the yen's rise was "Japan's problem and Japan should take care of it." \par \par \uc2  A stream of top U.S. monetary officials have made clear the dollar's decline against the yen isn't particularly worrisome, and there aren't any signs the U.S. would be willing to join in a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Without the threat of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Hillel Waxman, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Bank Leumi in New York, "it's is back to the market versus the Bank of Japan." \par \par \uc2  And what the market seems to want is a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  The market's resolve to bid the yen higher gained momentum Thursday after Japan's second-quarter gross domestic product was reported up 0.2% quarter-on-quarter, while most market players had expected a decline. \par \par \uc2  For many, the number confirmed Japan's recovery was in place - the upturn having been foreshadowed by a surprising 2.0% growth rate in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Japan's growth potential has been increasingly luring investors into the Nikkei benchmark 225 stock index, which has gained about 39% this year. That, combined with the natural draw of a country with a current account surplus, has reinforced the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The GDP number thus sent the dollar spiraling down three yen to a three-year low Thursday, bottoming out at Y107.55. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, worried that the yen's premature strength would damp the country's nascent recovery, stepped in and bought some $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly spooked market players who bid the dollar as high as Y110.00, but by midday Friday, the dollar had shed most of these gains, trading at Y108.88. \par \par \uc2  With such widespread yen demand, changing the direction of dollar/yen would require a concerted effort among Japan, Europe and the U.S. - a scenario that most analysts say is unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Analysts seem to agree that Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was therefore an attempt to smooth volatility and the rapid pace of the yen's strengthening, rather than to reverse its direction. \par \par \uc2  "Part of the market's reason in pushing the dollar lower against the yen was to determine if there was a point where it could get a reaction," said Sinche. "If there was not a reaction, the market might not have stopped pushing." \par \par \uc2  In a sense, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players seem to conclude that the move was a token gesture to let the market know Japanese officials were still watching and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still a possibility. \par \par \uc2  "I think they felt they had to put some two-way risk in the market," Sinche said. \par \par \uc2  And as long as that risk is there, trading should be choppy and cautious as the dollar approached lower levels. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The dollar will be caught in a bit of a dance in terms of the market\par wanting to push the yen higher and its concern about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said\par Nathaniel Litwak, chief trader at Credit Agricole in New York.\par -By Marianne Sullivan 201-938-4385\par email:marianne.sullivan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  10-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1851GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1146 4217 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010824dv9a00m97\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
