\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Close Mixed; Blue-Chip Nikkei Index Gains 33.46 Points \b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 10:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo stocks closed a bit higher Friday in narrowly mixed trading. \par \par \uc2  Japan's blue-chip Nikkei 225 index rose 33.46 points, or 0.2%, to close at 17711.02, adding to Thursday's 36.18-point gain. However, losers edged winners 7 to 6 while 1.17 billion shares were traded. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stock prices opened slightly lower but quickly rebounded as concerns about the strong yen subsided when the dollar jumped against the yen in Asia after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. A higher yen makes Japanese exports more expensive abroad and thus less competitive, cutting into earnings of export-oriented Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially sank further against the yen in early Asian trading, extending its three-year low hit in New York on Thursday. But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at stopping the yen's rapid rise, lifting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 109 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  However, the stock market lost steam in afternoon trading and shed most of the morning's gains on concerns that one-time \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be enough to keep the upward rise of the yen in check. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on the prospects for Tokyo stocks next week, Garry Evans, a strategist at HSBC Securities, said, "I think that stocks have a little bit farther to go." \par \par \uc2  Evans sees the Nikkei Average trading in a relatively narrow range between 17500 and 18000 next week. "Foreigners are still raising their Japan weightings and remain slight net buyers. I think we are likely to see more buying of stocks linked to domestic growth and some cyclical issues, while traders will remain jittery about blue chips and exporters," added Evans. \par \par \uc2  In afternoon trading, investors focused on individual issues following a slew of company announcements about planned \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  reorganizations. \par \par \uc2  Electronics giant NEC Corp. was by far the most actively traded issue Friday, up a whopping 239 yen to 2,160 yen. The stock finished above the 2,000-yen mark for the first time since 1990 following a report that the electronics giant has come up with a drastic restructuring plan. \par \par \uc2  Kirin Brewery Co. shot up late Friday afternoon after news that the major beer brewer was poised to unveil its group-reorganization plans. At the close Kirin Brewery was up 78 yen at 1,350 yen. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Takeda Chemical Industries Ltd., Daiichi Pharmaceutical Co. and Eisai Co. closed mixed Friday after the three drug companies said that they had agreed on cash payments to settle U.S. antitrust allegations. Takeda Chemical said that it will pay a fine of $72 million, Daiichi Pharmaceutical will pay $25 million, and Eisai will pay $40 million under a deal with the U.S. Department of Justice which alleged that six companies conspired to fix prices for vitamin products in the U.S. in the early 1990s. Takeda Chemical ended down 20 yen at 5,640 yen, Daiichi Pharmaceutical ended up 4 yen at 1,715 yen, and Eisai ended down 5 yen at 2,585 yen. The impact of the news was limited, as the stronger yen prompted investors to focus on issues less vulnerable to fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, market sources said. \par \par \uc2  Shares of textile manufacturers Teijin Ltd. and Toho Rayon Co. jumped Friday after major polyester maker Teijin said Thursday that it aims to acquire a majority stake in Toho Rayon through a tender-bid offer this month. Teijin ended 22 yen higher at 492 yen and Toho Rayon finished 12 yen higher at 192 yen. \par \par \uc2  Home builder Sekisui House Ltd. also moved higher as investors welcomed the company's plan to reevaluate its land holdings at current market levels to dispose of latent losses. Sekisui House shares ended at 1,210 yen, up 42 yen. The company said Thursday it will report a massive special loss of about 190 billion yen in a lump sum in the second fiscal half ending January 2000 as a result of the reappraisal of all its land assets held for sale. \par \par \uc2  On the over-the-counter market, shares of Doutor Coffee Co., which operates one of Japan's largest coffee shop chains, extended gains Friday. The stock closed up 700 yen at 9,600 yen. It also rose 700 yen Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9a03m2t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
