\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs surge in late Tokyo as yen scores fresh gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 09:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - March Japanese government bond (JGB) futures surged near Friday's close on speculative buying, drawing support from a renewed yen rise against the dollar despite Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the day. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said many traders were forced to liquidate short positions when the March contract made a decisive break above 130. \par \par \uc2  March JGBs closed 0.39 higher at 130.19. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the key 215th 10-year bond was at 1.795 percent, down from Thursday's close of 1.830 percent. \par \par \uc2  Turnover for the March contract was 31,724 lots. \par \par \uc2  "Some speculators may have bought up the contract on expectations that the yen would resume its strength," a trader at a trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  A rapidly strengthening yen, if left untamed, would hurt Japan's economic recovery and raise speculation that the Bank of Japan will keep adding ample liquidity to the money market. \par \par \uc2  The March contract initially gained to 130.00 in morning trade on the yen's surge to three-year highs against the dollar overnight. \par \par \uc2  But trade turned directionless after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the yen's rise. The BOJ's dollar-buying action, the first since July 21, pushed the greenback to a high of 111.00 yen from around 107.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 108.99/09 yen as of 0654 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The bond market lacks clear direction as it is caught between positive and negative factors," a trader at another trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  JGBs will likely be supported on cash-rich investor buying. But improving economic fundamentals, underscored by strong second-quarter growth, and upcoming bond supply will weigh on the market, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In the cash market, some selling in the 210th bonds by government-affiliated financial institutions was detected, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the medium-term sector was slightly weighed down by press reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would issue five-year interest-bearing bonds to finance the second supplementary budget. \par \par \uc2  A MOF official told Reuters on Friday that nothing had been decided on financing the budget. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Thursday he hopes to compile a supplementary budget by the end of November. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, budget requests for MOF's Trust Fund Bureau fell 38.1 percent to 8.39 trillion yen from 13.55 trillion yen a year earlier, the MOF said. \par \par \uc2  Less funds could raise speculation that the Trust Fund Bureau could take on fewer JGBs for the coming fiscal year. It currently purchases 200 billion yen worth of JGBs outright per month. \par \par \uc2  But market reaction to the news was limited as cuts in funding were widely expected, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The March TIBOR-based euroyen futures contract was last traded at 99.73, unchanged from Thursday's day-session settlement. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the BOJ reduced the net surplus in the money market as upward pressure on the key overnight call rate cooled with jitters of possible computers problems stemming from 9/9/99 having subsided. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ left the money market with a net surplus of 1.1 trillion yen after its regular 0020 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with 2.1 trillion yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The key overnight call rate was traded around 0.02 to 0.03 percent, down from Thursday's weighted average of 0.06 percent. \par \par \uc2  Three-month certificates of deposit were issued at 0.03 percent. ((Tamawa Kadoya, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00z2o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
