\par\par \b \uc2  Yen sent reeling by BOJ, but bulls undaunted. \b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 06:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The yen was sent reeling on Friday morning after Japan ambushed the market by buying dollars, but dealers and analysts doubted it would be down for long. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan struck in early trade after the yen had powered to three-year highs against the dollar overnight at 107.55 and a lifetime peak on the euro at 113.02. \par \par \uc2  It bought dollars through Japanese banks and the electronic broker system first around 107.70/80 and again around 109.00, sending the dollar scurrying as high as 110.00. \par \par \uc2  By 0330 GMT a dizzy dollar was taking in the view at 109.64/69 having climbed from 107.83/88 in New York on Thursday, which was its lowest finish since August 1996. \par \par \uc2  The euro also won a reprieve, skipping to 115.25/35 from 113.70 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a trade low of 113.02 overnight. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said this bore all the hallmarks of a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than an attempt to protect a particular level or to drive the yen lower in a sustained way. \par \par \uc2  "The main message from the policymakers is that they're opposed to a rapid movement," Ken Landon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "As long as it's a fundamentally based rally in the yen, and I believe policymakers believe that, they have no reason to fear it so much." \par \par \uc2  Landon argued the outcome would be similar to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, with the BOJ catching the market by surprise at first but the impact lessening with every subsequent round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Eventually, people wait for them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so they can sell dollars at higher levels," he said. \par \par \uc2  "At that point the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes totally ineffective and we make new lows. I think that will happen again; this will not change the trend." \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have also scaled back their ambitions, with no-one talking about keeping the yen lower as they did back in June and July when the BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to counter violent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements but mentioned no target levels for dollar/yen. He also conceded that U.S. assistance was unlikely, saying the yen's rise was Japan's problem alone. \par \par \uc2  And Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya illustrated why the yen was rising in the first place when he said that the economy looked brighter and should achieve a full recovery in the next fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Signs of a recovery in Japan this year have encouraged offshore fund managers to fatten the long-anaemic weighting of Japanese assets in their portfolios, with a resulting increase in inflows into the yen. \par \par \uc2  Data out this morning showed foreign investors bought a net 979 billion yen of Japanese equities in August on top of 1.18 trillion worth in July. \par \par \uc2  Japan's massive trade surplus is also fuelling yen strength with exporters keen to sell dollars to hedge their foreign earnings. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say many exporters had dragged their feet on this in the last few months in the hope the BOJ would drive the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  But recently the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been urging firms to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility on their own and dealers expected a lot more yen demand from this source ahead of the fourth quarter, particularly given liquidity concerns arising from the millennium computer bug problem. \par \par \uc2  Technically the dollar was also looking weak against the yen after breaching the January lows around 108.20 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were not too impressed by Friday's bounce, especially as it was only inspired by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and argued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to beat the week's high of 111.40 to generate some real support. \par \par \uc2  Their short-term target remained 105.65, which would be a 62 percent retracement of the entire uptrend from the record trough of 79.70 in April 1995 to last year's peak at 147.63. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had more luck against the euro, however, firming to $1.0510/20 from $1.0540/47 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  It was assisted by disappointing GDP and industrial production data from Germany this week and by the steady performance of U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00ypw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
