\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar supported by wariness of BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 14:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar stuck to a narrow trading range against the yen in Europe on Friday having failed to keep hold of overnight highs seen after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was more than 50 cents below Friday's high of 109.97, set after rallying of more than two percent from Thursday's four-month low, following BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Analaysts said near term the dollar would be supported by market nervousness that the BOJ might act again, coupled with expectations U.S. producer price index at 1230 GMT would help boost U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "Today the dollar will be helped by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the improvement in the bond market," said Rob Hayward, economic adviser at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "Assuming there is not anything too untoward in the PPI, we will probably have a slightly better tone for the U.S. asset markets and if we do see that we should see a slightly better tone for the dollar, particularly against the yen." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 109.23/33 yen by 1112 GMT, compared with Thursday's four-month low of 107.52. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year long bond was at 100.21 up 6/32 on the day. \par \par \uc2  Expectations are for U.S. August PPI to rise 0.3 percent month-on-month, 0.1 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  However, Hayward said benefits to the dollar should be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "The overall the impression that the Japanese economy is doing better, suggests that there will continue to be money going into Japan," he said adding there was little the BOJ could do to halt that. \par \par \uc2  Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London said Japanese and foreign investors were likely to view any artificial weakness in the yen as a good opportunity to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge foreign assets, "to hedge foreign assets in the case of Japanese investors and to increase their presence in Japanese equities in the case of foreign investors". \par \par \uc2  Japan's Prime Minister Obuchi said on Friday a rapid yen rise was undesirable. His comments echoed those of Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa who said overnight dollar/yen movements have been violent and the yen's rise was Japan's problem. \par \par \uc2  While Hayward did not expect the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, he said the overnight activity had introduced an air of caution which would probably lead to a more gradual appreciation of the yen. \par \par \uc2  "People will be looking at 108 (on dollar/yen) because the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came below there and it came after Miyazawa mentioned that level, but it was probably the speed at which it was falling yesterday that made them come into the market rather than any particular level," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the activity in dollar/yen had kept euro/dollar somewhat sidelined on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was last at $1.0527/32 after hitting $1.0486 earlier, its lowest in nearly two weeks. \par \par \uc2  The euro dipped briefly after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Eugenio Domingo Solans said euro stability was more important than euro strength and the euro would derive strength in the long term from its stability. \par \par \uc2  "Solans comments are probably not as dramatic as the headline implied," said Bank of America's Hayward. \par \par \uc2  "He is probably just suggesting they are happy with the way things are and that they are not interested in manipulating the exchange rate up and down a few cents here and there." \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc fell to its lowest in more than a week against both the euro and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank said on Friday it had not adopted an informal peg for the Swiss franc against the euro and said it would not rule out the possibility of allowing more volatility in euro/Swiss in the future. \par \par \uc2  "The SNB comments seem to suggest a calm attitude towards policy," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  "They are not pushing for an immediate change in interest rates and are probably going to follow any move from the ECB unless we get continued weakness. They seem to be pretty happy with anything around 1.60 (on euro/Swiss)." \par \par \uc2  Euro/Swiss was last at 1.6025/30, within 1/4 cent of 1.6031 its high so far in September hit earlier. ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year long bond was at 100.21 up 6/32 on the day. \par \par \uc2  Expectations are for U.S. August PPI to rise 0.3 percent month-on-month, 0.1 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  However, Hayward said benefits to the dollar should be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "The overall the impression that the Japanese economy is doing better, suggests that there will continue to be money going into Japan," he said adding there was little the BOJ could do to halt that. \par \par \uc2  Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London said Japanese and foreign investors were likely to view any artificial weakness in the yen as a good opportunity to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge foreign assets, "to hedge foreign assets in the case of Japanese investors and to increase their presence in Japanese equities in the ca