\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr higher on BOJ action but yen bulls undeterred. \b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar was higher against the yen by late afternoon on Friday after the Bank of Japan earlier bought an estimated $2 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since July 21. The BOJ was detected buying dollars near 107.80 yen early on Friday and near 109.30 yen later in the morning, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and other MOF officials later confirmed that the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen, and the dollar rose as high as 110.00 yen on the BOJ's action. \par \par \uc2  The dollar later slipped back, and was quoted at 109.05/10 yen as of 0618 GMT, against 108.00/10 yen in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.0530/34 as of 0626 GMT against $1.0530/35 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that movements in the dollar/yen rate had been "violent" and that he would seek to correct such moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  While he said he would not comment on appropriate levels for the dollar/yen rate, he said: "We will still move to correct violent movements." Dealers said Miyazawa's comments suggested that more BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take place in European and U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they would be especially alert for potential BOJ action at levels near 108 yen to the dollar, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was first detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday morning. \par \par \uc2  "I think a level of around 108 yen will serve as a temporary floor for the dollar," said a senior options dealer for a major city bank. Market players said they detected signs of a shift in Japanese authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, from a strategy seeking to push up the dollar's value towards a "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " aimed at slowing its descent. \par \par \uc2  "A smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only choice they have. It's futile to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the prevailing market trend for any other purpose," said Chiaki Ishii, treasury manager for the Bank of New York in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Yasuhito Kawashima, chief manager for Toyo Trust & Banking Corp's trading department, said: "I think it's a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... The cause of the yen buying is Japan's improving economic fundamentals, so I don't think Japanese authorities are displeased about the yen's rise itself." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said authorities were likely trying to give Japanese exporters some respite from recent rises in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Local exporters have not yet sold as many dollars as their commercial needs warrant, bankers said. "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at giving people who missed opportunities to sell the dollar above 110 yen another chance," said Bank of New York's Ishii. "But as is often the case when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes place, such people have begun to hope for another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are refraining from selling the dollar," Ishii said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese exporters seemed to be hoping to sell above 110 yen, with their dollar offers mostly lined up from 110 yen towards 111 yen. \par \par \uc2  In comments which seemed to confirm the market's views that Japanese authorities were not displeased about the yen's level itself, Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya said the current dollar/yen level will not damage \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy. Sakaiya added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at preventing volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the firmness of Tokyo share prices probably had much bearing on authorities' lenient view towards the rising yen. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's appreciation has not led to sustained falls in the Tokyo stock market," said Takayuki Togawa, manager for Tokai Bank's international trading department. \par \par \uc2  Unless Tokyo share prices were to fall sharply below 17,000 yen and towards levels seen at the end of the last fiscal year, Japanese authorities were unlikely to revert to aggressive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Togawa said. \par \par \uc2  The key Nikkei 225 average ended at 17,711.02 on Friday, up 33.46 from Thursday's close and nearly 12 percent from its March 31 closing level of 15,836.59. Earlier, MOF said foreign investors purchased a net 979.4 billion yen worth of Japanese stocks on a contract basis in August. \par \par \uc2  "The trend of yen appreciation is based on capital flows and is unlikely to abate" when taking into account the surprising 0.2 percent rise in Japan's April-June gross domestic product announced on Thursday, the Bank of New York's Ishii said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 114.52/71 yen as of 0720 GMT against 113.80 yen in late New York on Thursday. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00tp1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
