\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Summers reiterates support for strong dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers on Friday reiterated his support for a strong dollar, even as the greenback hovered near its lowest level against the Japanese yen in three years. \par \par \uc2  But in a by now familiar routine, he refused to endorse Japan's latest attempts on Thursday to reign in the recent rise of the yen against the dollar by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Instead, he stressed the need for sound economic fundamentals as the only route to market stability. \par \par \uc2  "Our policy and approach is unchanged - a strong dollar is very much in the interests of the United States," he told reporters after an appearance at the Treasury Department. "We believe in the appropriateness of a strong dollar." \par \par \uc2  He added he was not prepared to discuss "any possible plans or intentions we might have with respect to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  Summers' remarks came after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen for dollars in an attempt to cap the yen's recent rise, which Tokyo fears could undermine the country's frail economic recovery by making its exports more expensive abroad. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's action came after the yen hit three-year highs against the dollar amid mounting optimism over Japan's ability to finally pull its economy out of a deep slump. That sentiment was fuelled earlier in the week by reports that the world's second-biggest economy unexpectedly grew in the second quarter of the year. \par \par \uc2  But Summers signalled that he remains sceptical about the merits of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , indicating there is little chance that the United States is willing to back Tokyo's attempts. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that as a matter of general economic approach that what is right is not to seek to manage to markets but instead to concentrate on building the right fundamentals for our economies... Ultimately, we believe that what's most important to markets is the fundamentals," he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. policymakers have long viewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an instrument that is to be used sparingly at most, insisting that they prefer a much more hands-off approach to movements in financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The last time Washington deemed the time was right for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in June of last year - when it helped \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act on what was then seen as an overly weak yen. \par \par \uc2  But Summers' insistence on economic fundamentals has done little though to keep Japan from trying repeatedly to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in one way or another or to dampen what it regards as excessive and harmful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00tj9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
