\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar rises, but yen starts making a comeback. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 23:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar ended slightly firmer against the Japanese yen on Friday, but shed most of its overnight gains in late U.S. trade after the Bank of Japan failed to come in with a second round of yen-selling. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced higher overnight after the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars, rescuing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from three-year lows hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But the yen reasserted itself during the New York session, paring back more than half the dollar's gains and slamming the euro to a new lifetime low versus the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This (demand for yen) is not going to stop soon," Mellon Bank trader Grant Wilson said, noting the yen would need to fall below 110 dollars per yen to force a change of heart for the market. \par \par \uc2  Data showing Japan's economy grew 0.2 in the April to June quarter sparked the yen's strong rally on Thursday. Now even efforts by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the sharp gains through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to quell investor appetite. \par \par \uc2  The yen posted its biggest gains against the euro, gaining nearly 2-1/2 yen since early New York trading and forcing the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 112.74 yen, after it began its decent into record lows on Thursday. The euro then hit seven week lows against the dollar at $1.0349 before steadying back near $1.0365/75 in late U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  Since its January launch, the euro has lost over 16 percent in value against the yen and over 11 percent against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  While the yen took a bite out of the dollar's gains, the threat of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the market on its toes and held the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off its lows. \par \par \uc2  By late U.S. trading, the dollar stood more than a yen below its overnight highs at 108.69/79, down slightly from 109.10/20 yen early New York trading, but propped above late Thursday's 108.00/10 yen level. \par \par \uc2  As the session dragged on, traders became more sceptical about the chances for another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I doubt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come in again today," a dealer at a U.S. investment bank said, contradicting another trader who predicted thin pre-weekend trading conditions would offer a perfect opportunity for the Bank of Japan to strike again. \par \par \uc2  But there was little doubt the U.S. would remain sidelined in case of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Washington is not going to do a thing unless there are some really crazy moves in the market and they needed to smooth that out," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the morning, traders reported euro sales after the U.S. Labour Department reported mixed wholesale inflation data. \par \par \uc2  Sharp gains in energy and raw materials prices helped push August's producer price index up 0.5 percent, but the market focused more on the core rate, which contracted 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The core rate strips out volatile food and energy prices. Economists had forecast an overall gain of 0.3 percent following July's 0.2 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the euro were also reflected in Swiss franc trading where the greenback roared to its highest level in two months. In late U.S. trading, the dollar stood at 1.5510/20 francs, rising from 1.5234/45 francs early in the New York session and 1.5200/10 francs in late Thursday dealing. \par \par \uc2  The British pound extended its overnight losses, before finding it footing shortly after noon to stand at $1.6187/97 in late U.S. trading, from $1.6285/92 earlier and $1.6335/45 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar also slipped, falling to $0.6509/19 from $0.6522/32 and $0.6529/34 on Thursday. The dollar fell against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4757/67 after having risen to C$1.4801/11 at the start from C$1.4776/86. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00rw3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
