\par\par \b \uc2  Yen flexes muscle, hits lifetime high versus euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 19:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen flexed its muscles on Friday, sending Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a record low and reclaiming ground against the dollar as traders bet Japan's economic recovery may outpace growth in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Data showing Japan's economy grew 0.2 percent in the April to June quarter sparked the yen's strong rally on Thursday. And even efforts by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the sharp gains through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to quell investor appetite. \par \par \uc2  "This (demand for yen) is not going to stop soon," Mellon Bank trader Grant Wilson said, noting the dollar would have to climb above 110 yen to force a change of heart for the market. \par \par \uc2  The yen posted its biggest gains against the euro, gaining nearly two yen since early New York trading and forcing the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 113.10 yen, its second lifetime low in as many days. The euro then hit seven week lows against the dollar at $1.0379 before steadying back above the $1.04 level at noon. \par \par \uc2  Since its January launch, the euro has lost about 15 percent in value against the yen and over 11 percent against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  While the market was more cautious in trading yen against dollar as the threat of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loomed, the yen still took a chunk out of the greenback's overnight gains. By noon, the dollar had slipped to 108.92/02 from its 109.10/20 start. \par \par \uc2  As the session dragged on, traders became more sceptical about the chances for another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I doubt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come in again today," a dealer at a U.S. investment bank said, contradicting another trader who predicted thin pre-weekend trading conditions may offer a perfect opportunity for the Bank of Japan to strike again. \par \par \uc2  "Historically speaking there is never just one bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... I expect we will see them again today," said John Schein, vice president at Generale USA Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  But there was little doubt the U.S. would remain on the sidelines in case of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Washington is not going to do a thing unless there are some really crazy moves in the market and they needed to smooth that out," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the morning, softer than expected U.S. inflation data had helped spark some euro sales, because the data possibly reduces the need for another near-term U.S. interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Sharp gains in energy and raw materials prices helped push August's producer price index up 0.5 percent, but the market focused more on the core rate which contracted 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The core rate strips out volatile food and energy prices. Economists had forecast an overall gain of 0.3 percent following July's 0.2 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street also applauded the tame inflation news, sending the Dow Jones industrial average up over 35 points in early afternoon trading. Sustained gains may be helpful for the dollar, dealers agreed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the euro were also reflected in Swiss franc trading where the greenback roared to its highest level in two months. At noon it stood at 1.5448/58 rising from 1.5234/45 at the start and 1.5200/10 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound extended its losses, slipping to $1.6182/92 from $1.6285/92 and $1.6335/45. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar also slipped, falling to $0.6516/21 from $0.6522/32 and $0.6529/34 on Thursday. The dollar fell against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4740/50 after having risen to C$1.4801/11 at the start from C$1.4776/86. \par \par \uc2  ((-North American Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00rmk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
