\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar flies vs euro, flops vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 1,040 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar bounced against the euro but lost much of its overnight gain against the yen in Europe on Friday, as the impact of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo lessened. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to its highest level against the euro this month after U.S. core producer prices showed a surprise 0.1 percent contraction in August, but the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to match those gains against the unstoppable yen. \par \par \uc2  "The strength of the U.S. stock market is probably driving the dollar up against the euro more than anything else, but there are lots of reasons to buy the yen, even with BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Alfonso Prat-Gay, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose to two-week highs at 11142, amid optimism the U.S. economy was growing without inflationary pressures, before easing to 11064 by 1420 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0477/79 from a low of $1.0460, its weakest since August 30, and from $1.0553/55 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar slipped against the yen to 108.69/74, from overnight highs of 109.97 after the BOJ sold yen for dollars, and within sight of a three-year low of 107.52 set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the attractions of Japanese assets to both domestic and international investors were too great to ignore after two consecutive quarters of growth in Japan, and that the BOJ's yen sales on Friday were insufficient to stem the tide. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was estimated by dealers to have bought $2 billion for yen on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "If it wants to avoid the dollar falling fairly sharply to 105, the BOJ will have to come in again," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  The euro/yen cross rate bore the brunt of euro weakness and yen strength on Friday, falling to within 1/4 of a yen of life lows set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It was trading at 113.58/76 at 1433 GMT, from a low of 113.45 and from 114.36/66 in late European trade on Thursday. Traders said the euro/yen rate might be targeted by Japanese authorities in any further rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the market was showing too much optimism about orderly growth in the U.S. economy and too much pessimism about Europe, particularly as overall U.S. PPI, excluding food and energy, rose by 0.5 percent in August. \par \par \uc2  "The move in euro/dollar on the PPI is overdone," said Ferridge. \par \par \uc2  "Energy prices are as valid as anything else in terms of inflation - you cannot just take out oil." \par \par \uc2  Prat-Gay said the euro would target $1.08-$1.10 by the end of the year amid an upturn in the euro-zone economies, but it was hard to pinpoint the exact turning point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chartists see immediate euro support at $1.0390, the low set on August 24. \par \par \uc2  The pound rallied to three-month highs against the euro on Friday, boosted by Britain's surprise decision to raise interest rates by a quarter of a point to 5.25 percent on Wednesday, and by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on Thursday to keep euro rates steady at 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling was trading at 64.46/53 pence at 1458 GMT, from a low of 64.32 and from 64.61/67 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/dollar followed euro/dollar lower, dropping to $1.6228/38 from Thursday's four-month high of $1.6374. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the attractions of Japanese assets to both domestic and international investors were too great to ignore after two consecutive quarters of growth in Japan, and that the BOJ's yen sales on Friday were insufficient to stem the tide. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was estimated by dealers to have bought $2 billion for yen on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "If it wants to avoid the dollar falling fairly sharply to 105, the BOJ will have to come in again," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  The euro/yen cross rate bore the brunt of euro weakness and yen strength on Friday, falling to within 1/4 of a yen of life lows set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It was trading at 113.58/76 at 1433 GMT, from a low of 113.45 and from 114.36/66 in late European trade on Thursday. Traders said the euro/yen rate might be targeted by Japanese authorities in any further rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the market was showing too much optimism about orderly growth in the U.S. economy and too much pessimism about Europe, particularly as overall U.S. PPI, excluding food and energy, rose by 0.5 percent in August. \par \par \uc2  "The move in euro/dollar on the PPI is overdone," said Ferridge. \par \par \uc2  "Energy prices are as valid as anything else in terms of inflation - you cannot just take out oil." \par \par \uc2  Prat-Gay said the euro would target $1.08-$1.10 by the end of the year amid an upturn in the euro-zone economies, but it was hard to pinpoint the exact turning point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chartists see immediate euro support at $1.0390, the low set on August 24. \par \par \uc2  The pound rallied to three-month highs against the euro on Friday, boosted by Britain's surprise decision to raise interest rates by a quarter of a point to 5.25 percent on Wednesday, and by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on Thursday to keep euro rates steady at 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling was trading at 64.46/53 pence at 1458 GMT, from a low of 64.32 and from 64.61/67 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/dollar followed euro/dollar lower, dropping to $1.6228/38 from Thursday's four-month high of $1.6374. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00rel\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
