\par\par \b \uc2  Early Tokyo -2: Dlr Extends Losses Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 03:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The dollar dropped sharply to Y108.90 on massive offers from U.S. funds among others. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. funds actively sold the dollar after the GDP data release. Dollar/yen support is seen at 108.65, but the dollar might fall further below that level, triggering some stops," a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese city bank said. \par \par \uc2  The euro also plunged to Y115.50 in tandem with the dollar's sharp drop. \par \par \uc2  "The euro dropped sharply following the dollar's plunge. As market participants were surprised at Japan's April-June GDP data, more money might flow into Japan," a senior trader at a Japanese trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  Market attention was fixed on whether or not the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sharply rose to Y110.15 after the GDP- shock on market talk of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, some traders doubted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually took action, and added the moves were created by short-dollar covering by some speculative accounts. \par \par \uc2  "It was not due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. But market attention is still fixed on the BOJ and finance ministry," said a trader at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda said Thursday morning that the GDP data showed some bright signs, but declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  The MOF's International Bureau chief Zembei Mizoguchi said Thursday morning that the ministry would take proper action if needed. \par \par \uc2  The euro/dollar showed rather quiet moves between $1.0587 and $1.0618 amid drops both in the dollar/yen and euro/yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at Y115.80, down from Y117.08 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was quoted late in Tokyo Wednesday at Y117.68. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6210, up from $1.6185 in New York late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is seen trading between Y108.50-Y110.00, while the euro is expected to trade between $1.0580-$1.0630 for the rest of the Asian day. \par \par \uc2  -By Hidenobu Kageyama; 813-5255-2929; hkageyama@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 09-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0116GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9906llg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
