\par\par \b \uc2  Yen charges to new heights on Japan GDP strength. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Sternoff \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 19:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 9 (Reuters) - The yen rampaged higher on Thursday, to a lifetime high against the euro and a three-year peak against the dollar, as stronger-than-expected Japanese growth data sent traders scrambling to buy yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen surged three percent against the dollar and 3.6 percent against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as markets that had been braced for bad news out of Tokyo overnight were surprised to learn Japan's gross domestic product rose by 0.2 percent in April-June. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's just being bought across the board," said Ben Strauss, vice president of Bank Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro was hit harder than the dollar because weak German second-quarter GDP figures cast a shadow over the recent spate of rosy conomic forecasts for Europe. \par \par \uc2  "With Germany coming in with unchanged quarter-to-quarter GDP data and the Japanese number coming in strong, there is a glaring difference in the speed of recoveries," Strauss said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained extremely edgy the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put the brakes on the yen's rise, but they pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher after a top Federal Reserve official said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not affecting the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't believe the change in the dollar/yen exchange rates has particularly significant implications for the U.S. economy," said Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said McDonough's comments added weight to speculation that the United States is unlikely to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any possible future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has expressed concern that a strong yen could choke off recovery by making Japanese exports more expensive. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at three-year lows against the yen near 107.70 at midday in New York after starting at 108.82/92 as compared to 111.05/15 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro plumbed new depths against the yen to a low of 113.35 yen before bouncing back a bit, down from Thursday's session high at 117.60 yen, with traders citing heavy institutional selling in euro/yen. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lost a cent against the dollar and was trading below 65 pence in what traders said were the continuing effects of Wednesday's surprise interest rate hike by the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling continues to be bought on the back of yesterday's rate hike," Strauss said. "People are buying (pounds), selling the continental \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying English-speaking dollars in the crosses." \par \par \uc2  The British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading just above $1.63 after hitting a four-month peak at $1.6348. Sterling started in New York Thursday at $1.6285/95 against $1.6180/90 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold and its president Wim Duisenberg signalled that no move should be expected soon. \par \par \uc2  Traders became briefly excited when Duisenberg acknowledged that a tightening bias was creeping into the ECB's considerations at a "snail's pace", but the comment failed to lift the euro significantly before it slumped near $1.0550. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the greenback slipped against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4800/10 from C$1.4840/50 in early trading against C$1.4875/85 on Wednesday. The dollar edged up against the Swiss franc, trading at 1.5166/76 against 1.5100/10 and 1.5093/03 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar gained to $0.6523/33 from $0.6501/11 early Thursday and $0.6476/81 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (( Daniel Sternoff, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1690 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9900yg7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
