\par\par \b \uc2  Yen rules  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  markets after strong GDP data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 16:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 9 (Reuters) - The yen was king of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets early Thursday after stronger-than-expected Japanese growth data helped spark a two percent rally against the dollar and even sharper gains against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan's gross domestic product rose 0.2 percent in the April-June quarter, catching financial markets, which had been awaiting a contraction of about 0.3 percent, completely off guard. \par \par \uc2  After seeing the sharp drop in Japanese corporate capital spending, which was interpreted as a bad omen for the growth data on Tuesday, dealers had repositioned themselves and were then forced to quickly reverse direction again. \par \par \uc2  The yen climbed steadily, hitting an eight-month high against the dollar at 108.43 yen as New York trading got under way and coming within striking distance of its 114.73 life high against the euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said talk that a knock-out option may be protected near 108 yen could offer the dollar a foothold near those levels but otherwise many braced for further losses. \par \par \uc2  "There has been some logic to these yen moves. Until now there has been talk that the strong yen would snuff out economic growth in Japan, but that is obviously not the case," said Barclays Capital senior economist Henry Willmore. \par \par \uc2  With yen gains picking up speed again, the market returned to yellow alert for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Earlier this summer, a surge in first-quarter Japanese growth triggered a series of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And overnight senior Japanese officials stepped up warnings that abrupt moves in the yen's exchange rate would be met with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Still concerned about the durability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery, Japan's Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui said, "A rapid rise in the yen could have a negative impact on the Japanese economy and is not favourable." \par \par \uc2  "We will monitor movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully and take appropriate action if needed," he added. \par \par \uc2  The timing and force of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains in doubt, however, dealers agreed. "The market is pricing in good news and the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not want to fly in the face of the market moving so strongly in one direction," Mike Malpede, senior analyst at Refco Group Ltd said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also continue to mull whether the United States might assist Japan in any action, especially after Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers on Wednesday reconfirmed America's interest in a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Summers's commitment to that policy, closely associated with his predecessor Robert Rubin, has been questioned recently however, especially as analysts say he could be much more forceful in pushing that policy. \par \par \uc2  Consequently, many U.S. traders expect Washington to remain on the sidelines of any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , letting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fend for itself. \par \par \uc2  As New York trading got underway, the dollar started at 108.82/92 yen, off Wednesday's 111.05/15 closing price. Meanwhile the euro started off nearly unchanged at $1.0593/98 after ending at $1.0593/00 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Softer-than-expected German growth data, unchanged in the second quarter from the first quarter when it had grown 0.4 percent from the final quarter of 1998, exerted some pressure on the euro early. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold and its president Wim Duisenberg signalled that no move should be expected soon. \par \par \uc2  Traders became briefly excited when Duisenberg acknowledged that a tightening bias was creeping into the ECB's considerations at a "snail's pace". However the comment was not enough to propel the euro past resistance at $1.0650, leaving it closer to support near $1.0570. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound remained strong after Wednesday's unexpected interest rate hike to start at $1.6285/95 after $1.6180/90 on Wednesday. The dollar inched higher against the Swiss franc, reaching 1.5100/10 francs after 1.5093/03. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4840/50 from C$1.4875/85 while the Australian dollar gained to $0.6501/11 from $0.6476/81. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9900xfh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
