\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen surges, eyes 1999 high vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 09:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 9 (Reuters) - The yen rallied by two percent against the dollar and by nearly the same amount against the euro by the European open on Thursday, cheered by Japanese second quarter growth. \par \par \uc2  Japanese gross domestic product rose by 0.2 percent in the April-June quarter, against a forecast 0.35 percent contraction, encouraging investors looking for further signs of a recovering Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose against the dollar to within sight of its highest levels of 1999. \par \par \uc2  "We are waiting to test the January low (in dollar/yen)," said Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The capital flows are still looking very positive for the yen." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 109.04/09 yen at 0700 GMT, from an early European low of 108.89 and from an overnight high of 111.17, before the release of the data. \par \par \uc2  If the dollar breaks below the low of 108.62 yen set on September 1, it will fall to its lowest since January 11, when it traded at 108.11. \par \par \uc2  A break of that level would send the dollar to its lowest against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since August, 1996. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to one-week lows at 115.48 yen before recovering to 115.89/94 at 0708 GMT, around one yen above its life low of 114.73 set on August 31. \par \par \uc2  A surge in first quarter Japanese growth triggered a series of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June and July. \par \par \uc2  The market remains wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength, with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuing overnight. \par \par \uc2  Nobuaki Usui, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister, said Japan would take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi sould the authorities would continue to watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts saw the yen rally running out of steam without any help from the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "The GDP number was obviously better than expected, and it looks as if in the second quarter there was less of the hand of the public sector, so there is more of a self-sustaining recovery, that's clearly what's giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost," said Andrew Snowball, international economist at Julius Baer Investments. \par \par \uc2  "But I'm still not convinced the Japanese recovery will be smooth from here on in. These are old data. The numbers we've seen since then have been less encouraging. For choice, I would be a seller of yen at these levels." \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, eased against the dollar ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular policy meeting on Thursday, following disappointing German growth data. \par \par \uc2  Second quarter German GDP was unchanged on the quarter, against expectations for a 0.2 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the markets would take relatively little notice of these backward-looking data. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0613/18 at 0725 GMT, from a high of $1.0634 but up from $1.0592/97 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A poll of 39 Reuters economists released on Monday showed they were unanimous in expecting no rate change from Thursday's ECB meeting. \par \par \uc2  Sterling remained strong against the dollar after Wednesday's surprise British rate rise, trading at $1.6228/33 from $1.6196/206 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The pound is poised to break August highs around $1.63 and rally further, analysts said, as interest rate futures factor in British interest rates near seven percent by the end of next year, from the current 5.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9900vva\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
