\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen's bull run back at full speed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 17:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 9 (Reuters) - The yen's bull run was back at full speed on Thursday as a surprise rise in Japanese growth data propelled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to eight-month highs against the dollar and within reach of lifetime highs against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Japan's gross domestic product for the April-June quarter rose by 0.2 percent, following through on a 2.0 percent jump in the previous quarter and defying economists' forecasts of a small contraction. \par \par \uc2  The shock effect was amplified as the dollar had rallied sharply earlier in the week as weaker readings in some economic reports had prompted the market to lower their expectations for the GDP figures. \par \par \uc2  "With the GDP jitters gone, it looks like there's nothing that can stand in the yen's way short of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Minoru Ozawa, head of trading at Sakura Bank in London. "We have shaken out a lot of short dollar positions this week, so we have a clean slate for another push lower." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 108.87/92 yen at 1428 GMT down more than two percent from Wednesday's high of 111.27 yen. It briefly spiked down to a low of 108.43 yen, barely above an early January trough around 108.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 115.17/22 yen against Wednesday's 118.40 yen peak. It troughed at 115.00 yen in European trading, within about a quarter yen of the 114.73 yen life low set on August 31. \par \par \uc2  Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in London, said the dollar/yen market's focus was likely to return to when, or whether, Japanese authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a higher yen from stalling the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tentative economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities' stance on the yen hasn't changed. Despite the good GDP numbers, you could still say that a strong yen could starve off the recovery," he noted. "Short term, it really is dependent on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We certainly think they need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to turn dollar/yen around." The Japanese authorities have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for over a month, but there were plenty of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said overnight that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if dollar/yen movements were too rapid. His deputy, Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui said a rapid yen rise was not favourable for the Japanese economy and the ministry would take appropriate action if needed. \par \par \uc2  However, Federal Reserve Bank of New York President McDonough said the dollar/yen drop was not significant for the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro was weaker across the board, most notably against the pound, after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates steady at its regular monetary policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was last at $1.0564/69, down from around $1.06 before the ECB meeting ended and $1.0592/97 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling fell to a two-month low of 64.68 pence as sterling's interest rate edge came in focus after the ECB's no-move and the Bank of England's surprise 25-basis-point tightening in its key repo rate. \par \par \uc2  "What we're seeing is a followthrough from yesterday's (BOE) rate move," Metcalfe at Natwest said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there had been some speculation in the market that the ECB might follow the BOE's steps and raise rates today. \par \par \uc2  But ECB President Wim Duisenberg said at a news conference that the BOE move had no impact on ECB decisions and also remarked that the ECB was "less activist than some other central banks in the world tend to be" in fine-tuning the economy. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, sentiment on the euro was dented by weaker-than-expected second quarter German GDP, which was unchanged from the previous quarter against expectation of a modest 0.2-percent gain. \par \par \uc2  The demand for the pound spilled over to the sterling/dollar market, and the UK \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to a four-month high of $1.6348. Sterling was last trading at $1.6306/09 against $1.6196/06 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Metcalfe projected a target of $1.65 for sterling in the near term. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9900qga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
