\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro struggles after Welteke comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1999\par 18:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The euro was forced lower late in Europe on Tuesday, sheding quarter of a cent against the dollar immediately after Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke said an appropriate rate for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was $1.05, below current trading levels. \par \par \uc2  This was a revision to Welteke's comments earlier in the year that the Ecu's (European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit) early 1998 rate of $1.08 was the appropriate reference rate for the euro. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Welteke's remarks, coupled with continued mixed data from euro-zone economies, would restrict the upside for euro/dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If anything it removes any sort of potential upside (for the euro). It is another reason to think it won't go roaring up," said Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais in London. \par \par \uc2  Although euro/dollar was buoyed earlier on Tuesday by better than expected French second quarter gross domestic product numbers, he said the German second quarter GDP data on Thursday might not be quite so euro friendly. \par \par \uc2  "They could cause a bit of a re-think in the market," he said. "The euro will probably bounce around for a while." \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0585/86 by 1512 GMT, compared with a day's high of $1.0601 and with $1.0578/80 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters consensus of economists forecast German second quarter GDP to rise 0.2 percent quarter-on-quarter and 1.0 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  Welteke went on to say he saw no reason for the ECB to change interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said his comments on rates would have little impact on the market, given that few in the market were expecting a rate hike from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Just prior to Welteke's remarks preliminary data from the International Monetary Fund published on the Dutch finance ministry's website, raised forecasts for world economic growth to 2.8 percent from 2.3 percent for 1999 and to 3.4 percent from 3.3 percent for 2000. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's Euro-11 growth forecast for 1999 was unchanged at 2.1 percent and its forecast for 2000 was reduced to 2.8 percent from 2.9 percent previously. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. growth forecast for 1999 was revised up to 3.4 percent from 3.0 percent previously and Japanese 1999 growth was revised up to 0.2 percent from expectations of a 1.1 percent fall previously. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 110.80/85 yen compared with 109.62/72 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Europe the dollar firmed nearly two percent on the day to a high of 111.40 yen, but it retreated as concerns about U.S. assets resurfaced, with U.S. Treasuries down on the day and the Dow showing little follow-through on Friday's gains. \par \par \uc2  "With not much carry through from the strong gains made on Friday the (U.S. asset) markets are slightly nervous now," said Patrick O'Sullivan economist AIB Group Treasury in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  "They seemed to be happy enough after Friday's unemployment number that the Fed might not hike rates again but that was a premature decision to make." \par \par \uc2  O'Sullivan said as the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on October 5 approached, the prospects of another rate hike would increase. \par \par \uc2  "That should be reflected in weaker U.S. asset markets which will in turn hamper the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Davies at Credit Lyonnais said key for the yen would be Japan's second quarter GDP data, released at 2350 GMT on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There is a slight suspicion that it could be more negative than the market is thinking," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It would suit the Japanese to have a weak number because they are getting upset about yen strength." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said once the data were released, the way was still open for the yen to make further progress against the dollar, but the extent of any gains should be limited by concerns about the possibility of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Every time dollar/yen looks like breaking down into the 109s it heightens fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ and that brings it back up again," O'Sullivan at AIB said. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters consensus of economists forecast German second quarter GDP to rise 0.2 percent quarter-on-quarter and 1.0 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  Welteke went on to say he saw no reason for the ECB to change interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said his comments on rates would have little impact on the market, given that few in the market were expecting a rate hike from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Just prior to Welteke's remarks preliminary data from the International Monetary Fund published on the Dutch finance ministry's website, raised forecasts for world economic growth to 2.8 percent from 2.3 percent for 1999 and to 3.4 percent from 3.3 percent for 2000. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's Euro-11 growth forecast for 1999 was unchanged at 2.1 percent and its forecast for 2000 was reduced to 2.8 percent from 2.9 percent previously. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. growth forecast for 1999 was revised up to 3.4 percent from 3.0 percent previously and Japanese 1999 growth was revised up to 0.2 percent from expectations of a 1.1 percent fall previously. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 110.80/85 yen compared with 109.62/72 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Europe the dollar firmed nearly two percent on the day to a high of 111.40 yen, but it retreated as concerns about U.S. assets resurfaced, with U.S. Treasuries down on the day and the Dow showing little follow-through on Friday's gains. \par \par \uc2  "With not much carry through from the strong gains made on Friday the (U.S. asset) markets are slightly nervous now," said Patrick O'Sullivan economist AIB Group Treasury in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  "They seemed to be happy enough after Friday's unemployment number that the Fed might not hike rates again but that was a premature decision to make." \par \par \uc2  O'Sullivan said as the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on October 5 approached, the prospects of another rate hike would increase. \par \par \uc2  "That should be reflected in weaker U.S. asset markets which will in turn hamper the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Davies at Credit Lyonnais said key for the yen would be Japan's second quarter GDP data, released at 2350 GMT on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There is a slight suspicion that it could be more negative than the market is thinking," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It would suit the Japanese to have a weak number because they are getting upset about yen strength." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said once the data were released, the way was still open for the yen to make further progress against the dollar, but the extent of any gains should be limited by concerns about the possibility of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Every time dollar/yen looks like breaking down into the 109s it heightens fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ and that brings it back up again," O'Sullivan at AIB said. ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv97011cj\par }\page {