\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen in limbo,euro needs support in jaded Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1999\par 06:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The dollar was confined to an all too familiar trading range against the yen at the midsession on Tuesday as a jaded market awaited fresh impetus. \par \par \uc2  That may come with the return of U.S. investors from holiday later in the day, as any extension of Friday's surge in shares could lend the dollar support, mainly at the expense of the euro. \par \par \uc2  And it was the euro that was in need of support, with U.S. and European banks seen selling, possibly in an attempt to trigger stop-loss offers lurking under $1.0550 chart support. \par \par \uc2  In the event, the euro found just enough bids to hold the line, but dealers say much now depends on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  By 0345 GMT the dollar was idling at 109.67/77 yen, having hugged a 109.42/109.80 range all day after a 109.75/85 finish in London on Monday. Traders said it had found good demand from Japanese trust banks around 109.40 but U.S. investment banks were on the offer above 109.80. \par \par \uc2  The euro had inched up to $1.0557/67 from a $1.0540 morning low, but was still adrift of London's $1.0592/97. \par \par \uc2  The euro/dollar selling indirectly pushed it down on the yen to briefly threaten support around 115.50 yen, but dealers said actual trade on this cross had been marginal and it was holding at 115.78/99, down from 116.27 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said sentiment remained bullish on the yen but the market was concerned about the event risk surrounding Thursday's Japanese GDP data, given that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the previous release in June. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts emphasised that conditions were very different this time. In June, the report of a jump in January-March GDP growth, first reported at 1.9 percent, was a complete surprise to markets and drove the yen sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  This time Japanese officials have taken pains to prepare the market for a modest dip in April-June GDP, and analysts see very little risk of a result outside expectations. \par \par \uc2  If that proves true, the yen should derive some support from confirmation of the Japanese recovery story, but it should be nothing like the rush of buying seen after the January-March figures. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters predicted a drop in GDP of 0.3 percent from the previous quarter, an improvement from the 0.6 percent decline they forecast in late July. \par \par \uc2  The risk of concerted action against the yen also seemed more distant after Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he does not expect the Group of Seven meeting later this month to discuss joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Expectations for the GDP outcome could be further refined by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly survey of corporate sentiment and capital spending due out at 0600 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are concentrating on the result for capital expenditure which showed a surprising pick-up in the previous survey and led many to revise up their forecasts for January-March GDP, though none foresaw the scale of the actual increase. \par \par \uc2  They expect some pullback in this survey which should confirm their forecasts of a modest fall in GDP. \par \par \uc2  The survey of corporate sentiment is also a useful leading indicator of the key tankan survey, due on October 4, so a further improvement would bode well for that, they added. \par \par \uc2  The diffusion index for sentiment at large companies stood at minus 9.9 in the April-June quarter, compared with a revised minus 17.9 for January-March. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that activity may have been thinned further on Tuesday since any spot contract would be settled on Thursday which has the date 9/9/99. \par \par \uc2  This sequence of numbers could cause problems since in some computer languages it reads as an end-of-instruction order which could shut the computer down. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts emphasised that the languages involved are very old and any reasonably modern system would not be vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  Still with the market increasingly wary about potential liquidity problems ahead of the Y2K deadline, many banks were taking no chances, dealers said. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9700zrk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
