\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar makes little headway,eyes data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 1,260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1999\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 6 (Reuters) - The dollar made little headway against the euro or yen on Monday as the subdued market looked ahead to the key economic data due out later in the week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's early gains, helped by political concerns in Germany, and its pullbacks, were short-lived as a U.S. holiday kept momentum from building up in either direction. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market's focus was now on European economic data due out later this week, including Tuesday's German unemployment figures, and Thursday's Japanese growth data. \par \par \uc2  "Previous euro strength was determined by U.S. asset market corrections, but...I suspect that we're moving away from an asset play to a more cyclical play," Fabio Fraschetti, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banca Nazionale del Lavoro in London, said of the increased attention on European and Japanese data. \par \par \uc2  He said Friday's tame August U.S. employment report had taken the pressure off U.S. asset markets, which had been dogged by concern about a further Federal Reserve credit tightening. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0592/96 at 1426 GMT, little changed from $1.0596/98 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  It had dipped slightly in early Asian trading on damaging losses suffered by Germany's ruling SPD party in two state elections on Sunday. It also rose in Europe to a high of $1.0619 after stronger-than-expected German industrial orders data, but both moves quickly fizzled out. \par \par \uc2  Pan-German industry orders jumped 1.2 percent from June on a seasonally adjusted basis, well above expectations for a rise of only 0.1 percent. For Tuesday's August German unemployment data due out at 0800 GMT, economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a seasonally adjusted decline of 2,833 from July. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the most crucial data this week would be the Japanese gross domestic product for the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  Any signs that a whopping 2.0-percent quarter-on-quarter GDP jump in the January-March quarter was not just a hiccup could push the dollar below its January trough in the 108.20-yen area, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The only reason the dollar hasn't really gone through the floor is because of the risk of a surprise in GDP," said a Japanese bank dealer in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 109.62/72 yen, near the middle of the day's tight 109.35-110.02 range. \par \par \uc2  It had traded at 110.12/22 late in Europe on Friday and dropped early in Asia in sympathy with euro/yen, which was hit by the political setback for Germany's ruling party. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was last trading at 115.85/05 yen against 116.66/85 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the market will keep a close eye on the Japanese Finance Ministry's corporate sentiment and capital spending survey for the Apri-June quarter due at 0600 GMT, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovering economy was starting to wilt under the weight of a strong yen could prompt the Bank of Japan to step in. \par \par \uc2  "You have a potentially weak GDP, market position which is very long of yen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is too strong at this level," Fraschetti said. "If the catalyst of weak data comes into place, that would really put all conditions in place for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swedish crown rose to a life high of 8.6430 to the euro in early trade amid the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's broad weakness. Euro/Swedish crown had partially recovered by afternoon Europe, trading at 8.6568/18 against 8.6895/45 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was little changed on the day, reclaiming ground lost earlier on UK Foreign Secretary Robin Cook's remark about the Labour government's stance toward joining the euro. \par \par \uc2  Cook said the government would not let Britain "lose out by staying out" if the benefits that the euro member countries enjoyed now persisted. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was trading at $1.6046/56 after briefly testing support at $1.60, compared with $1.6037/47 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee was to start a two-day meeting on Tuesday, but most economists did not expect any changes in UK interest rates. ((London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Pan-German industry orders jumped 1.2 percent from June on a seasonally adjusted basis, well above expectations for a rise of only 0.1 percent. For Tuesday's August German unemployment data due out at 0800 GMT, economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a seasonally adjusted decline of 2,833 from July. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the most crucial data this week would be the Japanese gross domestic product for the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  Any signs that a whopping 2.0-percent quarter-on-quarter GDP jump in the January-March quarter was not just a hiccup could push the dollar below its January trough in the 108.20-yen area, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The only reason the dollar hasn't really gone through the floor is because of the risk of a surprise in GDP," said a Japanese bank dealer in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 109.62/72 yen, near the middle of the day's tight 109.