\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Modest dollar gains seen short-lived. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 957 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1999\par 13:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 6 (Reuters) - The dollar was trading in narrow ranges against the euro and the yen on Monday in a thin market due to the U.S. Labor Day holiday but any firmness in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to be temporary. \par \par \uc2  The dollar saw modest benefits after U.S. asset markets rallied following Friday's U.S. employment report. Analysts said the key economic focus for the week would be both Japanese and German second quarter GDP data on Thursday which are expected to boost the yen and euro respectively against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has obviously had some respite from what happened on Friday with the (U.S.) payrolls data, but it will be temporary. We are still looking for the dollar to go weaker," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  He said much of the momentum in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year had been from the euro and the yen and that was expected to continue to be going forward. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese GDP and German GDP will be interesting. Everything is still suggesting stronger growth in those countries and that will maintain momentum for a stronger yen and a stronger euro," Barrow said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0598/02 by 1114 GMT versus $1.0596/98 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar briefly extended its narrow day trading range just short of $1.0620 following better-than-expected German industry orders for July which rose 1.2 percent in July versus an expected rise of 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 109.73/76 compared with 110.12/22 in Friday's late European trade. \par \par \uc2  "Probably if there is a surprise on the Japanese GDP it will be for stronger data rather than weaker, so the yen could continue to be well bid," said Chris Iggo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  The market remained concerned the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may decide to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength. Various Japanese Finance Ministry officials spoke earlier on Monday reminding the market that Japan continued to watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and was prepared to take action if it was deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  "Friday's move beyond 110 (on dollar/yen) was mostly driven by hopes for some kind of (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the U.S. long weekend holiday. But if it doesn't materialise we should see a bit more pressure on dollar/yen tomorrow and we are likely to test this year's lows around 108.20," said Rottmann at HypoVereinsbank. \par \par \uc2  Barrow at Bear Stearns saw 108.00/20 as the key area for dollar/yen over the next few days and he said euro/dollar would rally towards the $1.08/$1.0825 zone, a return to its early August peak. \par \par \uc2  Analysts did not expect the losses suffered by Germany's ruling SPD party in two state elections on Sunday to have a lasting impact on euro/dollar. The SPD defeats were seen as highlighting problems the party is having with gaining acceptance for fiscal reforms within the budget. \par \par \uc2  The euro was slightly softer as European trade came in on Monday, setting a low for the day of $1.0578 as the market digested the results. \par \par \uc2  "It was always going to have been difficult for Schroeder to get through his reform package and this doesn't really change the outlook that much," said Iggo at Barclays. \par \par \uc2  "It is more the (European) economic data and whatever comments we get from the ECB after is policy meeting that are important." \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directorate meets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Probably if there is a surprise on the Japanese GDP it will be for stronger data rather than weaker, so the yen could continue to be well bid," said Chris Iggo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  The market remained concerned the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may decide to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength. Various Japanese Finance Ministry officials spoke earlier on Monday reminding the market that Japan continued to watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and was prepared to take action if it was deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  "Friday's move beyond 110 (on dollar/yen) was mostly driven by hopes for some kind of (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the U.S. long weekend holiday. But if it doesn't materialise we should see a bit more pressure on dollar/yen tomorrow and we are likely to test this year's lows around 108.20," said Rottmann at HypoVereinsbank. \par \par \uc2  Barrow at Bear Stearns saw 108.00/20 as the key area for dollar/yen over the next few days and he said euro/dollar would rally towards the $1.08/$1.0825 zone, a return to its early August peak. \par \par \uc2  Analysts did not expect the losses suffered by Germany's ruling SPD party in two state elections on Sunday to have a lasting impact on euro/dollar. The SPD defeats were seen as highlighting problems the party is having with gaining acceptance for fiscal reforms within the budget. \par \par \uc2  The euro was slightly softer as European trade came in on Mon