\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr jumps one yen in Tokyo before US payroll data. \b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 07:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped about one yen by Tokyo midday on Friday as short holders concentrated on covering their positions before the release of U.S. payroll data set for 1230 GMT and before the long weekend in the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar strengthened as high as 110.24 yen on bids led by U.S. fund operators. It also received support from the yen's steep falls against the euro, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's recovery was mostly technical. Recent sales in the dollar/yen were a bit overdone. Today was the best time to unwind short positions, but the dollar's basic trend is still bearish," a senior European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At 0318 GMT, the dollar stood at 110.11/19 yen compared with 109.10/20 yen in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. fund operators were detected actively buying the dollar against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese life insurers were spotted bidding in the euro against the yen, possibly to unwind some of their hedge sales positions. But some dealers said they may be buying for bargain-hunting following recent firmness in the euro. \par \par \uc2  At 0326 GMT, the euro stood at 117.63/80 yen from around 116.52 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market has become more wary recently over possible yen sales by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with vague talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  circulating in overseas markets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's upward momentum intensified slightly on comments by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. There is no need to hesitate," Miyazawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also said Japan does not need to wait for a meeting of the Group of Seven (G7) major nations later this month in Washington to seek cooperation from its partners to rectify a premature rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's recovery on Friday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still vulnerable amid lingering caution in U.S. asset markets after sharp losses overnight, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average fell 94.67 points or 0.87 percent to 10,843.21 on Thursday, and the key 30-year bond yield jumped to 6.13 percent from 6.08 percent. \par \par \uc2  All this stoked speculation the Fed may have to tighten further, a fear given credence by Federal Reserve Governor Edward Kelley's comment that it was premature to assume the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would wait until next year before raising rates. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said further declines in U.S. asset markets would likely drag the dollar lower, with attention closely focused on Friday's U.S. jobs data. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Reuters expect the data to show non-farm payrolls grew by 220,000 in August after 310,000 jobs were added in July. Unemployment is expected to have fallen to 4.2 percent from July's 4.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Technically, the dollar could rebound further should it break through key resistance of 110.60/70 yen," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But the dollar could come under fresh selling pressure and test (this year's low of) 108.21 yen if the jobs data spurs rate hike fears and triggers selling in U.S. bond and stock markets." \par \par \uc2  The euro moved little against the dollar, but maintained firm footing throughout the morning. \par \par \uc2  At 0402 GMT, the euro stood at $1.0682/87 against $1.0680/87 in late New York trade on Thursday. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9301bvf\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 17/10/1999\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clb